{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings*.md; cat docs/agents/roles/architect.md; rg --files /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab | rg 'antlr-multi|1792289'\"","aggregated_output":"     588 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings-v2.md\n     392 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md\n     980 total\n---\ndescription: Lead System Architect role definition for datom.world\n---\n\n# ROLE: Lead System Architect\n\n## Domain Scope\n\n- Foundational axioms, architectural invariants, and design contracts\n- Subsystem boundaries, interpreters, host isolation, and explicit causality\n- Cross-platform architecture, migration strategy, and compatibility boundaries\n- ADR coherence, domain vocabulary, and theoretical grounding where useful\n- Architecture review and sign-off for high-risk or cross-cutting changes\n\nThis role owns no permanent file list. Each task defines the artifacts it may\ninspect or change and any permitted expansion.\n\n## Core Responsibilities\n\n1. **Protect Invariants**: Strictly enforce the 6 non-negotiable invariants (no hidden global state, no implicit control flow, no raw callbacks, no shared mutable state, no layer collapsing, no assumed graphs).\n2. **Decompose Complexity**: When new requirements emerge, decompose them into append-only streams, explicit interpreters, and immutable datom representations.\n3. **Malleability Governance**: Ensure architecture adheres to bounded complexity, loose coupling, and high cohesion as defined in [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../malleability.md).\n4. **Lineage Alignment**: Keep designs grounded in Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp architectural lineage.\n\n## Delegation Prompt Template\n\n```text\nCreated-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCreated-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n\n# Task: <Task Name>\n\nRole: Lead System Architect\n\nImplementers:\n- Model: <model-name> | Assigned: <local timestamp> | Status: active | Rationale: <why>\n\nPerform a read-only architecture review of <design-change>.\n\nRead first:\n- docs/design/datom.world.md\n- <target-design-file>\n- <relevant-implementation-boundary>\n- <relevant-contract-tests>\n\nEvaluate foundational invariants, ownership boundaries, explicit state and\ncontrol flow, concurrency and linearization, dynamic extension, host isolation,\nCLJ/CLJS/CLJD portability, migration risk, completion criteria, and design\ncontradictions. Distinguish architectural defects from implementation gaps or\nintentionally deferred work. Do not edit files.\n\nBegin the final response exactly with:\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n\nThen report: severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | recommended correction.\nAlso confirm the requested properties that passed review.\n```\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792292000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792292000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792299000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-re-review.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792292000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-review.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792299000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-re-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792294000000-architect-antlr-multi-reconcile.claude-cloud-fable.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-engineer-antlr-multi-target.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792294000000-architect-antlr-multi-reconcile.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792291000000-engineer-antlr-multi-m012.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings-v2.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792291000000-engineer-antlr-multi-m012.claude-sonnet-5-5.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792282000000-engineer-antlr-multi-target.prompt.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792292000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings-v2.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792292000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings-v2.md'","aggregated_output":"I’ll check the plan against the architecture rules, landed TS/PHP designs, parser code, and runtime APIs, then write severity-tagged findings with file-and-line evidence.\n\nThe existing packet validator checks tree links, but it does not enforce all the properties the plan attributes to it. I’m checking which gaps the proposed `conform` can close, plus the JS/Dart runtime APIs and how languages with missing targets would load.\n\nI found three concrete problems: the parity test omits spans and synthetic flags; generation is not ordered before the REPL builds that will import its output; and the add-a-language recipe leaves unconditional imports pointing at omitted targets. The JS code-point stream is real: ANTLR 4.13.2’s `CharStreams.fromString` enables it.\n\nCompleted-GMT / Completed-Local\n2026-10-17 12:52:02 GMT / 2026-10-17 19:52:02 +0700\n\nThe existing CST v1 is a sound choice for the shared IR. Sign-off is blocked by gaps in its conformance contract, the build dependency graph, and the promised handling of languages with missing targets.\n\n1. **[P1] The conformance oracle does not establish the seven invariants.**  \n   [Plan:82–103](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:82) attributes preorder and tree enforcement to `validate!`. I reproduced two violations that it accepts: duplicate child edges `[1 1 2]`, and an index-ordered tree whose actual DFS preorder is `[0 1 3 2]`. The cause is visible in [packet.cljc:25–41](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/src/cljc/yang/antlr/packet.cljc:25).\n\n   The proposed source-only `conform` also cannot establish that rule/token names came from the grammar tables. Its listed checks omit real-token text equality and UTF-8 boundary alignment; byte bounds alone accept offsets inside a multibyte character. Meanwhile, `validate!` rejects legitimate error packets because they have no nodes.\n\n   **Required:** define outcome-specific validation; enforce actual DFS preorder and unique edges; check real-token text against source bytes, UTF-8 boundaries, terminal ordering, and exact rule extents over real descendants. Supply grammar metadata for name checks. Specify terminal monotonicity separately from nested rule spans.\n\n2. **[P1] The existing “precompiled packets” cannot satisfy M1 or prove M2 packet equality.**  \n   [Plan:245–248,350](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:245) says widening the existing test establishes packet parity and requires `conform` to pass every precompiled packet.\n\n   However, [python_antlr_test.cljc:340–355](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/test/yin/repl/python_antlr_test.cljc:340) compares only selected node fields, excluding spans and synthetic flags. Its packet builder deliberately assigns every span `[0 0]`, omits synthetic flags, and uses grammar `\"hand\"` ([lower_portable_test.cljc:11–37](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/test/yang/python/antlr/lower_portable_test.cljc:11)). These are lowering fixtures, not parser-output goldens.\n\n   **Required:** preserve those fixtures for lowering tests and add genuine JVM-exported packets with full metadata, edges, spans, and synthetic flags. Specify normalization or equality rules for error diagnostics too; checking only “error packet, no nodes” does not establish cross-target diagnostic parity.\n\n3. **[P1] Omitting a manifest target produces missing imports, not `unavailable-parser`.**  \n   [Plan:283–301](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:283) promises that omitting a target is sufficient. But the prescribed parser namespace imports generated recognizers on all three hosts, and the catalog requires that namespace before selection occurs. The current composition is statically wired in [frontends.cljc:11–18,72–80](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc:11).\n\n   **Required:** design the compile-time availability mechanism: conditional imports/profiles and installation, generated registration, or equivalent explicit composition. Demonstrate a Java-only language coexisting with Python in Node and Dart builds. An informational `:targets` set cannot resolve an import that fails before runtime.\n\n4. **[P1] Generation is not ordered before the builds that consume it.**  \n   [Plan:201–204](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:201) adds generation dependencies to test tasks. The actual recognizer consumers run in prerequisite tasks: `build:yin-repl-node` and `build:yin-repl-peer` ([bb.edn:6–38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/bb.edn:6)). Neither build depends on generation. Making generation another sibling prerequisite does not express the required dependency.\n\n   **Required:** attach generation to the consuming build tasks, update slow/all lanes and remaining task-name references, and cover both `:cljs` and the separate `:cljs-yin-repl` classpaths ([deps.edn:39,67–72](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/deps.edn:39)). Test clean direct builds, not only test wrappers.\n\n5. **[P1] The worker-isolation fallback contradicts the governing design.**  \n   [Plan:129–133](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:129) permits shared mutable caches when a host is single-threaded and caches are “read-mostly.” [yang.antlr.md:581–588](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:581) instead requires isolation by worker, process, classloader, or instance-owned caches.\n\n   Single-threaded execution prevents simultaneous access; it does not establish exclusive ownership or independence from previous requests.\n\n   **Required:** remove that fallback. Record the exact cache replacement or isolation mechanism per runtime and include interleaved-worker verification.\n\n6. **[P1] M3’s prerequisites omit a live consumer of the file it deletes.**  \n   [Plan:337–343](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:337) allows deletion after test-scenario disposition and REPL parity. The compilation demo still requires `yang.python` and calls `py/compile` ([compilation_pipeline.cljs:50,158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/src/cljs/datomworld/demo/compilation_pipeline.cljs:50)). Those prerequisites can all pass while deletion breaks the browser build.\n\n   **Required:** add a consumer inventory and demo migration before retirement opens. PHP’s landed design already makes demo retargeting an explicit retirement prerequisite ([yang.php.md:1291–1306](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/docs/design/yang.php.md:1291)).\n\n7. **[P2] The browser bundle already reaches the catalog.**  \n   [Plan:373–376](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:373) assumes ANTLR enters Node/test builds only unless the browser requires it. That require path already exists: browser demo → `yin.repl.driver` → `yin.repl.frontends` ([yin_repl.cljs:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/src/cljs/datomworld/demo/yin_repl.cljs:38), [driver.cljc:22](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:22)).\n\n   **Required:** explicitly choose browser inclusion or a separate composition, then add browser compile acceptance and measure its bundle impact. Removing the catalog gate makes this an immediate M2 consequence.\n\n8. **[P2] The Dart interoperability budget needs an executable acceptance gate.**  \n   The small per-language profile remains plausible; dynamic warnings do not inherently require duplicating the exporter per language. But [Plan:213–229](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:213) places `:cljd` after `:clj` in its examples despite its own ordering rule, and leaves the generated-parser call untyped.\n\n   Also, the supposedly portable lexical scan contains Java string calls such as `.charAt` ([repl_probe.clj:59–68](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/src/clj/yang/python/antlr/repl_probe.clj:59)). Purity does not make those calls portable.\n\n   **Required:** make M0 compile/import/construct a recognizer through actual CLJS and CLJD, with an explicit policy for dynamic interop warnings. Include portable string access in the probe port. Give M1 its own fixture profile rather than leaving its dependency on the M2 parser migration implicit.\n\n9. **[P2] Reconciliation and dependency provenance need corrections.**  \n   The broad reconciliation is valid: section 17’s lifts concern lowering, publication, and REPL infrastructure; new host exporters complement them. But [Plan:274–277,319–330](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:274) overstates the simplification:\n\n   - TS specifies Java/C#/C++ helpers, plus dialect and prediction-mode requirements—not literally Java-only upstream helpers ([yang.typescript.md:504–518](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/docs/design/yang.typescript.md:504)).\n   - PHP explicitly vendors its MIT Java helper. Preserve that exception to “vendor `.g4` only” ([yang.php.md:1319–1331](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/docs/design/yang.php.md:1319)).\n   - The Dart runtime includes MIT notices alongside BSD text; “runtimes are BSD-3-Clause” is incomplete. Record the complete [published license](https://pub.dev/packages/antlr4/license).\n   - Require lockfile updates and enforce manifest/runtime-pin consistency; descriptive runtime strings do not enforce it.\n\n   The mechanical recipe must also state its eligibility condition: target-portable grammar actions and tested helpers. The governing design explicitly says grammars-v4 does not guarantee cross-target compatibility ([yang.antlr.md:535–540](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4hang/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:535)).\n\nTwo challenged risks are **not blockers by themselves**: JS code-point input is concretely supported by [ANTLR 4.13.2 `CharStreams.fromString`](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/antlr/antlr4/4.13.2/runtime/JavaScript/src/antlr4/CharStreams.js), and Dart documents compatibility resolution for null-safe packages with a `<3.0.0` SDK ceiling ([migration guide](https://dart.dev/resources/dart-3-migration#dart-3-backwards-compatibility)). Retain the Unicode fixture and actual project-resolution checks.\n\nReview was read-only. The validator counterexamples were executed; proposed JS/Dart builds were not.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES     1\t# Plan: reconcile the antlr-multi architect plan against the codex review\n     2\t\n     3\t## Context\n     4\t\n     5\tThe prompt `collab/1792294000000-architect-antlr-multi-reconcile.prompt.md` asks the\n     6\tArchitect to revise the plan in\n     7\t`collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md` in place,\n     8\tanswering codex's REQUEST CHANGES review\n     9\t(`collab/1792292000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`: six P1,\n    10\tthree P2). The deliverable is a document, not code. Nothing under `src/`, `test/`, `bb.edn`,\n    11\t`deps.edn` or `docs/` changes in this round; the engineer round builds from the revised plan.\n    12\t\n    13\tAll nine findings were checked against the current tree (master `548f9faa`; the review's\n    14\tworktree `datomworld-c4hang` is on the same base) and all nine hold. Evidence per finding is in\n    15\tthe ledger below and goes into the document verbatim. Nothing was executed; the two validator\n    16\tcounterexamples were re-derived by reading `packet.cljc:25-41`.\n    17\t\n    18\t## Action (one step)\n    19\t\n    20\tOverwrite `collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md`\n    21\twith the \"Revised document (v2)\" below, verbatim. Keep the file name (the reviewer re-reviews\n    22\tthe same path). The final chat response starts with the two `Completed-GMT / Completed-Local`\n    23\tlines as the prompt requires. No commit (collab/ is never committed).\n    24\t\n    25\t## Verification\n    26\t\n    27\t- `git status` shows only that collab file modified; no tracked file touched.\n    28\t- The document contains a \"Delta vs v1\" section listing every section that changed, and a\n    29\t  ledger entry per finding (1-9) with concede/rebut and file:line evidence.\n    30\t- Re-read the ledger against the review once: every \"Required\" clause has a corresponding\n    31\t  design change in the named section.\n    32\t\n    33\t---\n    34\t\n    35\t# Revised document (v2)\n    36\t\n    37\t```markdown\n    38\t# ANTLR multi-target plan v2: one grammar, one parse-tree IR, N hosts, any language\n    39\t\n    40\tArchitect plan answering `collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.prompt.md`,\n    41\trevised against the codex review\n    42\t`collab/1792292000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n    43\t(REQUEST CHANGES: P1 x6, P2 x3) per\n    44\t`collab/1792294000000-architect-antlr-multi-reconcile.prompt.md`. It still gates the\n    45\tengineer round `collab/1792282000000-engineer-antlr-multi-target.prompt.md`; no worktree\n    46\t`datomworld-antlr-multi` exists yet.\n    47\t\n    48\tThe owner's requirements are unchanged and govern every revision below: (R1) ANTLR\n    49\tworks for JS and Dart before the legacy compiler retires; (R2) adding a new ANTLR\n    50\tlanguage stays mechanical.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 0. Reconciliation ledger (for the re-review)\n    53\t\n    54\tEvery finding is conceded on evidence; three carry a qualification. Section numbers\n    55\tpoint at the revised design.\n    56\t\n    57\t| # | Sev | Verdict | Evidence | Revised in |\n    58\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    59\t| 1 | P1 | **Conceded.** `validate!` checks only `i < k` and a `seen` set that is not updated between one node's children (`packet.cljc:34-38`): `[1 1 2]` and the index-ordered non-preorder tree both pass; the final count check (`:39-41`) cannot tell. Error packets have no `:yang.cst/nodes`, so `validate!` throws \"no root\" on a legitimate error packet (`:23-24`). A source-only `conform` cannot check names against grammar tables and v1's listed checks omitted real-token text and UTF-8 boundaries. | §1.2, §1.3 |\n    60\t| 2 | P1 | **Conceded.** `packets` in `python_antlr_test.cljc:44-49` are built by the `packet` helper of `lower_portable_test.cljc:11-37`: every span `[0 0]`, no `:synthetic`, grammar `\"hand\"`. `node-shape` (`python_antlr_test.cljc:340-344`) drops `:span` and `:synthetic`. These are lowering fixtures; v1 wrongly called them parity goldens. | §5.1, §5.2, §5.4 |\n    61\t| 3 | P1 | **Conceded.** `frontends.cljc:17-18` requires the parser namespace statically, and v1's `parser.cljc` (§4) required a generated recognizer on every host. An informational `:targets` set cannot stop a require that fails at compile time (on Dart a missing `package:` import fails the whole cljd compile). | §4.2, §6, §7 |\n    62\t| 4 | P1 | **Conceded, with a qualification.** `test:cljs` depends only on `build:yin-repl-node` and `test:cljd` only on `build:yin-repl-peer` (`bb.edn:33-38`); both builds compile `yin.repl.frontends` and neither depends on generation. Qualification: babashka runs `:depends` in declaration order, so `test:clj`'s sibling `[build:yin-repl-node gen:python-antlr]` (`bb.edn:30`) works today only because `build:yin-repl-node` does not yet need the generated code. v2 attaches generation to the consuming build tasks and covers the `:cljs-yin-repl` alias (`deps.edn:67-72`), which has its own `:extra-paths`. | §3.4 |\n    63\t| 5 | P1 | **Conceded.** v1 §2 allowed a shared read-mostly cache on single-threaded hosts; `yang.antlr.md` §4.3 (\"Generated recognizers may use shared caches... isolate them by worker, process, or classloader, or provide instance-owned caches\"; \"Merely constructing a fresh parser is insufficient evidence\") admits no such fallback. Both runtimes expose instance-owned caches, so no fallback is needed. | §2.3 |\n    64\t| 6 | P1 | **Conceded.** `compilation_pipeline.cljs:50` requires `yang.python` and `:158` calls `py/compile` for the demo's `:python` branch; v1 §10's preconditions would all pass while deleting `yang/python.cljc` breaks the `:demo` and `:local` browser builds. `yang.php.md:1296-1306` already makes demo retargeting a retirement prerequisite for PHP. | §10 |\n    65\t| 7 | P2 | **Conceded.** `yin_repl.cljs:38` requires `yin.repl.driver`, `driver.cljc:22` requires `yin.repl.frontends`; the `:demo`/`:local` browser builds (`shadow-cljs.edn`) therefore reach the catalog. Removing the gate puts the runtime and generated parser into the browser bundle at M2. v2 decides browser inclusion explicitly. | §6.2 |\n    66\t| 8 | P2 | **Conceded.** v1 §4 listed `:clj` before `:cljd` in its own example against its own rule. `repl_probe.clj:59-68` uses `.charAt` and `.startsWith`; `.charAt` is not Dart `String` API, so purity did not make `scan` portable. M1 depended implicitly on M2 for a profile to construct recognizers with. | §2.4, §4.1, §11 |\n    67\t| 9 | P2 | **Conceded.** TS upstream helpers are Java/C#/C++ with dialect and prediction-mode requirements (`yang.typescript.md:504-512`); PHP vendors its MIT Java helper beside the `.g4` (`yang.php.md:1319-1325`); the pub `antlr4` licence page carries MIT notices beside the BSD text; descriptive runtime strings enforce nothing; grammars-v4 does not guarantee cross-target compatibility (`yang.antlr.md:535-540`). | §3.1, §3.5, §7, §9 |\n    68\t\n    69\tTwo risks codex judged non-blocking stay as fixtures only: JS code-point input\n    70\t(`CharStreams.fromString` in antlr4 4.13.2 decodes to code points) and Dart's acceptance\n    71\tof a null-safe `<3.0.0` package under SDK `>=3.7.0`; both are verified by M0's executable\n    72\tacceptance (§11), not assumed.\n    73\t\n    74\t## Delta vs v1 (what the reviewer should re-read)\n    75\t\n    76\t- New: §0 ledger; §1.2 outcome-specific `validate!`; §1.3 `conform` against source and\n    77\t  vocabulary; §2.3 isolation mechanism per runtime, fallback removed; §2.4 portable probe;\n    78\t  §3.4 build ordering and both classpaths; §3.5 pin enforcement and lockfiles; §4.2 missing\n    79\t  target mechanism with a Java-only demonstration; §5 rewritten (genuine goldens, diagnostic\n    80\t  parity rules); §6.2 browser decision; §7 eligibility condition; §10 consumer inventory and\n    81\t  demo migration; §11 resequenced (M0 executable acceptance, `parser.cljc` moves to M1,\n    82\t  browser and demo work in M2).\n    83\t- Unchanged: the ruling that the IR is CST packet v1 (§1.1), the exporter contract (§2.1),\n    84\t  the manifest shape apart from `:runtime` (§3.1), the boundary (§8), the owner\n    85\t  requirements, the lanes policy.\n    86\t- Corrected statements: §9 (TS/PHP helper facts, vendoring exception, licences).\n    87\t\n    88\t## Context (unchanged from v1 except where marked)\n    89\t\n    90\t`(lang :yang.python/antlr)` works on the JVM only. The generator\n    91\t(`src/dev/yang_antlr_gen.clj`, `bb gen:python-antlr`) produces the Java target only,\n    92\t`src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj` is the only exporter, and `src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc`\n    93\tinstalls the ANTLR frontend under `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)`. Node and Dart answer\n    94\t`unavailable-parser` through `yin.repl/frontend-binding` (`repl.cljc:1696-1706`): a\n    95\tselected frontend whose binding is not usable throws the diagnostic. That mechanism is\n    96\treused in §4.2.\n    97\t\n    98\tOwner rulings (verbatim): \"don't remove legacy until antlr works for JS and dart too\";\n    99\t\"future languages will be added too and whatever is design should make it easy to add new\n   100\tantlr supported languages\"; \"maybe an antlr intermediate representation is needed if antlr\n   101\tdoesn't have that concept already.\"\n   102\t\n   103\tFacts (verified against the pinned sources; v1's list, two corrections marked):\n   104\t\n   105\t- grammars-v4 `python/python3` at `8af0d4c2` ships helper bases for Java, JavaScript,\n   106\t  Dart, C#, C++, Go, Python3, TypeScript; `desc.xml` tests `Cpp;CSharp;Java;JavaScript;Python3`.\n   107\t  The Dart helper is not exercised upstream.\n   108\t- The JS and Dart lexer bases carry the same NEWLINE/DEDENT `commonToken` construction the\n   109\t  Java patch fixes; the patch is ported per target.\n   110\t- JS helpers: ESM, `import antlr4 from 'antlr4'`, circular import with the generated parser.\n   111\t  Dart helpers: `package:antlr4/antlr4.dart`, `dart:io`. No licence notice in either.\n   112\t- Runtimes: npm `antlr4` 4.13.2 (BSD-3-Clause, ESM, node>=16); pub `antlr4` 4.13.2 (SDK\n   113\t  `>=2.12.0 <3.0.0`, deps `logging`, `collection`). **Corrected (finding 9):** the pub\n   114\t  package's published licence combines BSD-3-Clause with MIT notices for bundled parts; the\n   115\t  manifest records the full published text at pin time, not \"BSD-3-Clause\".\n   116\t- ANTLR has no target-neutral tree; the repo's neutral form is CST packet v1\n   117\t  (`cst.clj:16-35`), read through `packet.cljc`. **Corrected (finding 2):** the hand-built\n   118\t  packets of `lower_portable_test.cljc` and `python_antlr_test.cljc` are lowering fixtures\n   119\t  (zero spans, no synthetic flags, grammar `\"hand\"`), not parser goldens.\n   120\t- TS (`yang.typescript.md` 5.3) and PHP (`yang.php.md` 6) are JVM-only for helper\n   121\t  availability reasons: TS has Java/C#/C++ bases upstream (plus dialect-flag and\n   122\t  prediction-mode requirements of its own); PHP has Java (vendored, MIT), JavaScript,\n   123\t  C#, C++, Python3, Antlr4ng bases and no Dart one.\n   124\t\n   125\t## The design in one paragraph (unchanged)\n   126\t\n   127\tANTLR gives \"one grammar, N generated recognizers\". The repo adds the other half: one\n   128\ttarget-neutral parse-tree IR that every host's exporter produces and every language's\n   129\tlowering consumes. That IR is CST packet v1, now formally owned by `packet.cljc` with an\n   130\toutcome-specific validator and a conformance oracle. A language is: a manifest (grammar,\n   131\tper-target helpers, patches, digests, runtime pins), a ~40-line `parser.cljc` with one\n   132\treader-conditional branch per generated target, its lowering over the IR, one catalog\n   133\t`install` line, and its goldens. Nothing per language touches the generator, the exporter,\n   134\tthe packet reader, or the REPL.\n   135\t\n   136\t## 1. The IR: `:yang.cst/v1`, formalized\n   137\t\n   138\t### 1.1 Home and ruling (unchanged)\n   139\t\n   140\tThe IR is the existing packet shape, not a new schema. `packet.cljc` owns it: the shape\n   141\tdoc at `cst.clj:16-35` moves there verbatim; `packet-version` moves there. D1 (the\n   142\tdiagnostic namespace) stays with lift L2 of `yang.typescript.md` 17.\n   143\t\n   144\t### 1.2 Structural validation is outcome-specific (revised, finding 1)\n   145\t\n   146\t`packet/validate!` is rewritten; its contract is per outcome:\n   147\t\n   148\t- Every outcome: `:yang.cst/version 1`, `:yang.cst/outcome` in\n   149\t  `#{:yang.cst/ok :yang.cst/syntax-error :yang.cst/source-error}`, `:yang.cst/complete true`,\n   150\t  `:yang.cst/grammar` a string, `:yang.cst/unit` present. Unknown outcome: malformed.\n   151\t- `:yang.cst/ok`: `:yang.cst/root 0`; `:yang.cst/nodes` a non-empty vector; no\n   152\t  `:yang.cst/errors`. **Preorder, uniqueness and reachability are established by one\n   153\t  recursive walk** from node 0 with a counter: visiting node `i` requires `(:id (nth nodes\n   154\t  counter)) = counter = i`, then walks its `:children` in order, each child's id being the\n   155\t  counter at the moment it is visited; after the walk the counter equals `(count nodes)`.\n   156\t  This rejects duplicate edges (the second visit finds the counter past the id), the\n   157\t  index-ordered non-preorder tree (`[0 1 3 2]`: node 1's child must be 2), forward\n   158\t  references, and unreachable nodes, in one pass. A rule node's `:children` is a vector\n   159\t  (possibly empty); a token node has no `:children`. `:kind` in `#{:rule :token}`; rule\n   160\t  nodes carry a string `:rule`, token nodes a string `:type` and string `:text`; every node\n   161\t  a `[s e]` span of two non-negative integers with `s <= e`.\n   162\t- `:yang.cst/syntax-error` and `:yang.cst/source-error`: no `:yang.cst/nodes`, no\n   163\t  `:yang.cst/root`; `:yang.cst/errors` a non-empty vector of records whose `:kind` is\n   164\t  `:syntax-error` (line >= 1, column >= 0, span nil or `[s e]`) or `:source-error`, each with\n   165\t  a string `:message`.\n   166\t\n   167\tA legitimate error packet therefore validates. The tests `validate!` gains: the two codex\n   168\tcounterexamples, an error packet, a token with children, a rule without `:children`.\n   169\t\n   170\t### 1.3 `conform`: content against source and vocabulary (revised, finding 1)\n   171\t\n   172\t`packet/conform` takes `packet`, the sealed `source` string, and a `vocabulary`\n   173\t`{:rule-names #{...} :token-names #{...}}`. It runs after `validate!`. Returns the packet\n   174\tor throws a qualified `malformed-cst` naming the first violated invariant. Checks, for\n   175\t`:yang.cst/ok`:\n   176\t\n   177\t1. **Names**: every `:rule` is in `:rule-names`; every `:type` is in `:token-names` or is\n   178\t   `\"EOF\"`. The vocabulary is exported by each host (`cst/vocabulary worker`, §2.1), so the\n   179\t   check is against the generated tables of the running host; cross-host name-set\n   180\t   equality is a separate golden (§5.1).\n   181\t2. **Byte bounds and UTF-8 boundaries**: let `B` be the set of byte offsets that start a\n   182\t   code point in `source`, plus `utf8-length`. Every span end is in `B`. (Portable: build\n   183\t   `B` by walking code points with `utf8-length` as `cst.clj:68-88` does; no host string\n   184\t   internals.)\n   185\t3. **Real-token text**: for a token without `:synthetic`, `s < e` and the source bytes at\n   186\t   `[s e]` decode to exactly `:text` (decode via the code-point index that `B` gives;\n   187\t   compare strings).\n   188\t4. **Terminal ordering** (stated separately from rule spans): walking tokens in preorder,\n   189\t   each real token's `s` is >= the previous real token's `e`; a synthetic token has `s = e`\n   190\t   and sits at an offset >= the previous real token's `e` and <= the next real token's `s`.\n   191\t5. **Rule extents over real descendants**: a rule's span equals `[min s, max e]` over the\n   192\t   real tokens in its subtree; a rule with no real descendant is zero-width at an offset\n   193\t   between its neighbouring real tokens (rule 4 applied to the empty rule's position).\n   194\t6. **Synthetic flag is justified**: a token marked `:synthetic` either has text different\n   195\t   from the source at its position or would overlap the previous real token; a token not\n   196\t   marked has text equal to the source at its span (this is check 3 from the other side,\n   197\t   so an exporter cannot hide a misplaced token behind the flag).\n   198\t\n   199\tFor error outcomes: each `:syntax-error` span, when present, satisfies 2; `:line`/`:column`\n   200\tare consistent with the span (the line containing byte `s` and the code-point column within\n   201\tit, computed from `source`). For `:source-error`: nothing beyond `validate!`.\n   202\t\n   203\tNot checked by `conform`: that the root's end equals `utf8-length` (false when the source\n   204\tends in untokenized trivia); fixtures assert it where true.\n   205\t\n   206\t`conform` costs nothing at runtime (tests only).\n   207\t\n   208\t### 1.4 Normative invariants (restated; they are what §1.2-1.3 check)\n   209\t\n   210\t1. Ids are DFS-preorder indexes, root 0, children a tree (§1.2 walk).\n   211\t2. `:rule` and `:type` come from the host's generated tables (§1.3.1); `\"EOF\"` for EOF.\n   212\t3. Spans are half-open UTF-8 byte offsets on code-point boundaries (§1.3.2); each target\n   213\t   converts its own index unit at the boundary.\n   214\t4. A real token's text is the source at its span; a synthetic token is zero-width (§1.3.3, 6).\n   215\t5. Terminals are ordered and non-overlapping; rule spans cover real descendants (§1.3.4, 5).\n   216\t6. A syntax error yields an error packet with errors and no nodes (§1.2).\n   217\t7. `:yang.cst/grammar` names grammar revision and tool; the target does not appear in the\n   218\t   packet, in node fields, or in diagnostic text (§5.3).\n   219\t\n   220\t## 2. The exporter: `yang.antlr.cst` on three hosts\n   221\t\n   222\t### 2.1 Contract (v1 plus one export)\n   223\t\n   224\t`cst.clj` stays. `cst.cljs` and `cst.cljd` implement the same public names: `make-worker`,\n   225\t`parse-unit`, `parse-transform`, `utf8-bytes`, `first-failure` (§2.4), and **`vocabulary`**\n   226\t(new): `{:rule-names #{...} :token-names #{...}}` from the worker's parser (`ruleNames`,\n   227\tsymbolic and literal names). Profile contract unchanged:\n   228\t`{:grammar-id :make-lexer :make-parser :start}`.\n   229\t\n   230\t`parse-transform` and `source-error` are host-free and move to a cljc helper (engineer's\n   231\tcall on the namespace; minimal diff), so each host file holds only the host walk.\n   232\t\n   233\t### 2.2 Host walks (unchanged from v1)\n   234\t\n   235\tNode: `CharStreams.fromString` (code points), `CommonTokenStream`, an `ErrorListener`\n   236\tsubclass, `ctx.children`, `ctx.ruleIndex`, `parser.ruleNames`, `symbolicNames`/`literalNames`,\n   237\t`node.symbol.{type,start,stop,text}`. Dart: `InputStream.fromString`, `CommonTokenStream`,\n   238\t`BaseErrorListener`, `childCount/getChild`, `TerminalNode.symbol`, `ruleNames`, `vocabulary`.\n   239\tRecorded ClojureDart traps apply (reader-conditional ordering, `for` over chunked seqs,\n   240\tmulti-key `assoc` on nil, unary minus, protocol `[_ _]`).\n   241\t\n   242\t### 2.3 Worker isolation: the mechanism per runtime, no fallback (revised, finding 5)\n   243\t\n   244\tThe v1 fallback (\"document that the host is single-threaded and the cache is read-mostly\")\n   245\tis removed. `yang.antlr.md` §4.3 requires isolation; both runtimes support the JVM's\n   246\tmechanism exactly:\n   247\t\n   248\t| Runtime | Instance-owned caches | How the worker installs them |\n   249\t|---|---|---|\n   250\t| JVM (today) | `DFA[]` per decision, `PredictionContextCache` | `setInterpreter` with `LexerATNSimulator`/`ParserATNSimulator` (`cst.clj:242-258`) |\n   251\t| antlr4 npm 4.13.2 | `DFA` array, `PredictionContextCache` exported | `lexer._interp = new LexerATNSimulator(lexer, atn, decisionToDFA, cache)`, same for the parser's `_interp` |\n   252\t| antlr4 pub 4.13.2 | `DFA`, `PredictionContextCache` | `lexer.interpreter = LexerATNSimulator(lexer, atn, decisionToDFA, cache)`, same for the parser |\n   253\t\n   254\t`make-worker` on each host builds fresh DFA arrays and caches from the recognizer's ATN\n   255\t(as `cst.clj:100-124`); `parse-unit` installs them on every fresh lexer and parser. The\n   256\tgenerated recognizers' static shared caches are never used by a worker's parse.\n   257\t\n   258\tVerification (in `yang.antlr.conformance`, every host): an **interleaved-worker test**:\n   259\tworkers `w1`, `w2`; parse A on `w1`, B on `w2`, A' (a variant of A that exercises other\n   260\tdecisions) on `w1`, then A on `w2`; every packet equals the packet a fresh worker gives\n   261\tfor the same source; `w1`'s and `w2`'s DFA arrays and caches are distinct objects\n   262\t(`identical?` false); and on the JVM, Node and Dart alike the recognizer's static\n   263\t`_decisionToDFA`/`_sharedContextCache` is not the object installed on the worker. If a\n   264\truntime's generated code reaches its static cache through a path the worker cannot\n   265\tredirect, that is a blocking M1 finding, not a documented exception.\n   266\t\n   267\t### 2.4 Probe (revised, finding 8)\n   268\t\n   269\t`scan`/`probe` move to `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/repl_probe.cljc` **with portable string\n   270\taccess**: `(nth text i)` for a character, `subs` plus `=` (or `clojure.string/starts-with?`\n   271\ton a `subs`) for the closer test, `count` for length; no `.charAt`, `.startsWith`,\n   272\t`.offsetByCodePoints`. The host-specific `first-failure` becomes a `yang.antlr.cst` export\n   273\t(`first-failure worker source` answering `nil | :end | :early`) per host. The probe's\n   274\texisting JVM tests become cljc and run on three hosts (a scan-golden table is portable by\n   275\tconstruction).\n   276\t\n   277\t## 3. The build: one manifest, N targets\n   278\t\n   279\t### 3.1 Manifest (v1 shape; `:runtime` becomes structured, finding 9)\n   280\t\n   281\tAs v1, with these corrections:\n   282\t\n   283\t```clojure\n   284\t:targets\n   285\t{:java       {:runtime {:maven \"org.antlr/antlr4-runtime\" :version \"4.13.2\"} ...}\n   286\t :javascript {:runtime {:npm \"antlr4\" :version \"4.13.2\"} ...}\n   287\t :dart       {:runtime {:pub \"antlr4\" :version \"4.13.2\"} ...}}\n   288\t:license {...  \"antlr4 4.13.2 (npm)\" \"BSD-3-Clause (LICENSE.txt at pin)\"\n   289\t          \"antlr4 4.13.2 (pub)\" \"<full published licence text summary: BSD-3-Clause plus the MIT notices it carries>\"\n   290\t          \"JavaScript/Python3LexerBase.js\" \"no notice; UNRESOLVED\" ...}\n   291\t```\n   292\t\n   293\tHelper files are fetched by digest into `build/antlr/<grammar>/upstream` as today. The\n   294\t\"vendor `.g4` only\" rule has one recorded exception: a helper with an explicit licence\n   295\tnotice may be vendored beside the `.g4` with its notice (PHP's `Java/PhpLexerBase.java`,\n   296\t`yang.php.md:1319-1325`); the manifest's `:files` entry says `:vendored` either way, as\n   297\ttoday's generator already supports.\n   298\t\n   299\t### 3.2 Patches, digests, landing dirs (unchanged from v1)\n   300\t\n   301\tPer-target `:patches` (positioned-token port for JS and Dart), per-target `:generated`\n   302\tdigests and `generated-digests.edn`, ASCII check per target. Java byte-identical. Node:\n   303\t`build/antlr/python3/js` with ESM under `yang/antlr/gen/python3/`; Dart: `lib/antlr/python3`\n   304\t(`lib/` is gitignored, package root `datomworld_cljd`).\n   305\t\n   306\t### 3.3 Generator loop (unchanged)\n   307\t\n   308\t`yang_antlr_gen.clj` loops over `:targets`; `-Dlanguage=` is the per-target switch; the\n   309\tpackage-clause prepend and `javac` stay Java-only.\n   310\t\n   311\t### 3.4 Build ordering and classpaths (revised, finding 4)\n   312\t\n   313\t- `bb.edn`: `gen:python-antlr` becomes `gen:antlr` (all grammars, all targets,\n   314\t  `--grammar`/`--target` filters). **`:depends [gen:antlr]` is attached to the consuming\n   315\t  build tasks**: `build:yin-repl-node` and `build:yin-repl-peer`. `test:clj`,\n   316\t  `test:all:clj`, `test:slow:clj` keep a direct dependency too (the JVM lane loads classes\n   317\t  without a build task). The `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` families inherit through the builds.\n   318\t  Every remaining `gen:python-antlr` reference (docs, `build-n-test.md`) is renamed.\n   319\t- `deps.edn`: `build/antlr/python3/js` is added to **both** `:cljs` and `:cljs-yin-repl`\n   320\t  `:extra-paths` (`deps.edn:39,67-72`); the `:dev` alias too, since `watch demo` runs\n   321\t  through it. Dart needs no path: generated files are package sources.\n   322\t- Acceptance is a **clean direct build**, not a test wrapper: from a clean `build/` and\n   323\t  `lib/antlr`, `bb build:yin-repl-node` and `bb build:yin-repl-peer` each succeed\n   324\t  on their own, and `clj -M:cljs-yin-repl` starts.\n   325\t\n   326\t### 3.5 Pins, lockfiles, consistency (revised, finding 9)\n   327\t\n   328\t- `package.json` gains `\"antlr4\": \"4.13.2\"` (exact) and `package-lock.json` is updated in\n   329\t  the same commit; `pubspec.yaml` gains `antlr4: 4.13.2` and `pubspec.lock` likewise.\n   330\t- `gen:antlr` **enforces** that for every target in the manifest the runtime pin equals\n   331\t  the installed/locked version: Maven via the `:antlr-gen` classpath (as today), npm via\n   332\t  `package-lock.json`'s resolved `antlr4` version, pub via `pubspec.lock`. A mismatch fails\n   333\t  generation with the three versions printed. Tool and runtimes remain one release.\n   334\t\n   335\t## 4. The parser namespace per language\n   336\t\n   337\t### 4.1 Shape (ordering corrected, call typed; finding 8)\n   338\t\n   339\t```clojure\n   340\t(ns yang.python.antlr.parser\n   341\t  (:require [yang.antlr.cst :as cst] [yang.stage :as stage]\n   342\t            #?@(:cljd [[\"package:datomworld_cljd/antlr/python3/Python3Lexer.dart\" :as l]\n   343\t                       [\"package:datomworld_cljd/antlr/python3/Python3Parser.dart\" :as p]]\n   344\t                :cljs [[\"antlr4\" :as antlr4]\n   345\t                       [\"/yang/antlr/gen/python3/Python3Lexer.js\" :default Python3Lexer]\n   346\t                       [\"/yang/antlr/gen/python3/Python3Parser.js\" :default Python3Parser]]\n   347\t                :clj  []))\n   348\t  #?(:clj (:import (yang.python.antlr.gen Python3Lexer Python3Parser))))\n   349\t\n   350\t(def grammar-id \"grammars-v4/python/python3@8af0d4c2... antlr-4.13.2\")   ; one string, every host\n   351\t\n   352\t(def profile\n   353\t  {:grammar-id  grammar-id\n   354\t   :make-lexer  #?(:cljd (fn [cs] (l/Python3Lexer cs))  :cljs (fn [cs] (Python3Lexer. cs))  :clj (fn [cs] (Python3Lexer. cs)))\n   355\t   :make-parser #?(:cljd (fn [ts] (p/Python3Parser ts)) :cljs (fn [ts] (Python3Parser. ts)) :clj (fn [ts] (Python3Parser. ts)))\n   356\t   :start       #?(:cljd (fn [^p/Python3Parser p] (.file_input p)) :cljs (fn [p] (.file_input p)) :clj (fn [^Python3Parser p] (.file_input p)))})\n   357\t```\n   358\t\n   359\t`:cljd` first in every conditional (recorded trap). The Dart branch types the parser\n   360\targument so the entry call is static. `make-worker`, `parse-source`, `open-stage`,\n   361\t`step-stage` unchanged.\n   362\t\n   363\t### 4.2 Hosts without a target: explicit composition (new, finding 3)\n   364\t\n   365\tA language's `parser.cljc` has exactly one `:require` branch and one profile branch per\n   366\ttarget in its manifest `:targets`. For a host whose target is absent, the branch is\n   367\t**absent from the require form** (`#?@(:cljd [] ...)`, so no import is attempted; on Dart a\n   368\tmissing `package:` import is a compile failure, which is why nil-valued vars are not\n   369\tenough) and `profile` is `nil` on that host:\n   370\t\n   371\t```clojure\n   372\t(def profile #?(:cljd nil :cljs nil :clj {...}))     ; a Java-only language\n   373\t```\n   374\t\n   375\tThe catalog installs through one helper in `frontends.cljc`:\n   376\t\n   377\t```clojure\n   378\t(defn- antlr-install [catalog manifest profile binding]\n   379\t  (frontend/install catalog manifest (if profile binding (dissoc binding :parse))))\n   380\t```\n   381\t\n   382\tA binding without `:parse` is not usable, so selecting the language answers\n   383\t`unavailable-parser` through the existing path (`repl.cljc:1696-1706`); the engineer\n   384\tconfirms `usable-binding` answers nil for it (otherwise install `{}`). The frontend\n   385\tmanifest's `:yang.grammar/targets` set is **derived** from the grammar manifest's\n   386\t`:targets` keys at generation time (the generator writes `build/antlr/<g>/targets.edn`,\n   387\tread by a macro or copied as data; engineer's call), and a per-language test asserts on\n   388\teach host `(= (contains? targets <host-target>) (some? parser/profile))`, so the three\n   389\tsources (manifest, parser branches, catalog) cannot disagree silently.\n   390\t\n   391\t**Demonstration (M2 acceptance)**: a test-only Java-only language,\n   392\t`test/yang/javaonly/antlr/parser.cljc`, whose `:clj` branch reuses the Python3 Java\n   393\trecognizers and whose other branches are absent, installed beside Python in a test\n   394\tcatalog on all three hosts: the JVM parses through it; Node and Dart compile, build, and\n   395\tanswer `unavailable-parser` for it while `:yang.python/antlr` parses. No new grammar is\n   396\tneeded for the demonstration.\n   397\t\n   398\t## 5. CST equivalence: the parity story (rewritten, findings 1, 2)\n   399\t\n   400\t### 5.1 Genuine goldens, exported by the JVM\n   401\t\n   402\t`src/dev/yang_antlr_goldens.clj` (the `dht-import-v1.edn` pattern) writes\n   403\t`test/yang/antlr/fixtures/python3-goldens.edn`: for each fixture source, the **complete**\n   404\tpacket the JVM exporter produces (every node field, edges, spans, synthetic flags,\n   405\t`:yang.cst/grammar`, errors), plus `python3-vocabulary.edn` (the JVM's rule-name and\n   406\ttoken-name sets). The corpus: the four session lines of `python_antlr_test`, the sources\n   407\tof every `cst_export_test` case (`x = 1\\n`, the `é𝄞` line, the indentation case, the two\n   408\tmissing-final-newline cases, `def f(:` and `x = $`), the owner's comprehension line, and\n   409\tan empty source. Regenerating is a `bb` task; a drift in the committed file is a\n   410\treviewable diff.\n   411\t\n   412\t### 5.2 Three layers\n   413\t\n   414\t1. **Exporter conformance (per target, language-free)**: `yang.antlr.conformance` (cljc\n   415\t   test helper) takes a profile, the goldens, and the vocabulary. On the running host, for\n   416\t   each fixture: `validate!` and `conform` (against the source and the host's own\n   417\t   `cst/vocabulary`) pass; the host's packet **`=` the full golden packet**; the host's\n   418\t   vocabulary sets `=` the golden sets. Plus the interleaved-worker test (§2.3) and the\n   419\t   UTF-8 fixture asserting root end `= utf8-bytes` where the fixture ends in a real token.\n   420\t   `cst_export_test.clj` becomes `.cljc` and runs on three hosts through it.\n   421\t2. **Packet parity (per language)**: `precompiled-packets-are-the-parsers-test` is split:\n   422\t   (a) the hand-built lowering fixtures' `node-shape` equals the golden packets'\n   423\t   `node-shape` (fixtures stay what they are, lowering inputs; this pins them to real\n   424\t   parses); (b) on every host the installed parser's packet `=` the golden for each\n   425\t   session line. `langs` becomes `[:yang.python/cst :yang.python/antlr]` on all hosts.\n   426\t3. **Program parity (end to end)**: the REPL session scenarios, the prompt-suppress\n   427\t   regression and the owner's comprehension line run through `:yang.python/antlr` on Node\n   428\t   and Dart; expected output is the JVM's. Layer 2 implies 3 because the lowering is\n   429\t   portable and already proven on packets; layer 3 is what the owner sees.\n   430\t\n   431\t### 5.3 Diagnostic parity rules (new, finding 2)\n   432\t\n   433\tFor error packets the cross-target contract is `=` on the whole packet after\n   434\t**message normalization in the exporter**: `:kind`, `:line`, `:column`, `:span`, error\n   435\tcount and order are compared exactly; `:message` is compared after each exporter applies\n   436\t`packet/normalize-message` (trim, collapse internal whitespace, normalize the runtime's\n   437\ttoken-display quoting to ANTLR's Java form `'x'`/`<EOF>`). Where a runtime's\n   438\t`DefaultErrorStrategy` wording still differs, the exporter rewrites to the Java wording\n   439\t(invariant 7: the target does not appear in the packet). A fixture pair exists for a parser\n   440\terror (`def f(:`) and a lexer error (`x = $`), so both listener paths are covered. If a\n   441\twording cannot be normalized mechanically, that is an M1 finding to the Architect, not a\n   442\trelaxed fixture.\n   443\t\n   444\t### 5.4 Fixtures kept as they are\n   445\t\n   446\t`lower_portable_test.cljc`'s `packet` helper and `python_antlr_test`'s `packets` remain\n   447\tlowering fixtures (zero spans, `\"hand\"`); nothing asks `conform` to pass them. v1's claim\n   448\t\"conform passes every precompiled packet\" is withdrawn.\n   449\t\n   450\t## 6. Catalog and SPI changes\n   451\t\n   452\t### 6.1 Gate removal (as v1)\n   453\t\n   454\t`frontends.cljc` loses the `#?(:cljd [] :clj ...)` wrappers; the ANTLR frontend installs\n   455\ton every host through `antlr-install` (§4.2). Revision \"f3\" bumps; the grammar map gains\n   456\t`:yang.grammar/targets` (derived, §4.2). No SPI bump. Docstrings flip. Doc amendments as\n   457\tv1 §6 plus `yin.repl.md:48-70` (the \"JVM only\" paragraph).\n   458\t\n   459\t### 6.2 Browser inclusion: decided (new, finding 7)\n   460\t\n   461\tThe browser builds (`:demo`, `:local`) reach the catalog via `driver.cljc:22`, so after\n   462\t6.1 they include the npm runtime and the generated JS parser. **Decision: include it.**\n   463\tReasons: R1 asks for ANTLR on JS; the compilation demo must stop calling the legacy\n   464\tcompiler before M3 (§10), and an in-browser parser is the simplest retarget; the repo is\n   465\tdev-only. Acceptance at M2: `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release demo` compiles\n   466\tand the main module's minified size before and after is recorded in the M2 report.\n   467\tFallback, chosen before merging if the delta exceeds about 1.5 MB minified: a separate\n   468\tcomposition, where `driver` takes the catalog from the host and the browser passes one\n   469\twithout the ANTLR entry (the frontend is then installed with no `:parse`, §4.2, so the\n   470\tbrowser REPL answers `unavailable-parser` honestly) while the demo keeps the legacy\n   471\tpath until a packet-based retarget (PHP's B8.9 pattern) lands.\n   472\t\n   473\t## 7. Adding a language after this lands (the mechanical recipe; findings 3, 9)\n   474\t\n   475\t**Eligibility condition** (stated up front): the grammar's embedded actions are\n   476\ttarget-portable or absent, and a helper base exists and is tested for each target you\n   477\tclaim; grammars-v4 does not guarantee cross-target compatibility (`yang.antlr.md:535-540`),\n   478\tso a helper without upstream CI (Dart today) is accepted only through the goldens of §5.\n   479\t\n   480\tFor language L with grammar G:\n   481\t\n   482\t1. `antlr/<G>/manifest.edn`: vendor the `.g4` files (and any helper that carries its own\n   483\t   notice, §3.1); list helpers per target, patches, licence rows, runtime pins. A target\n   484\t   without a helper is **omitted from `:targets`**.\n   485\t2. `bb gen:antlr --grammar <G>`; record digests; the pin check passes.\n   486\t3. `src/cljc/yang/<L>/antlr/parser.cljc`: one require branch and one profile branch per\n   487\t   target in `:targets`; absent targets have no branch and `profile` nil there (§4.2).\n   488\t4. The lowering over the IR through `packet/*` only (§8).\n   489\t5. Goldens: sources for `yang_antlr_goldens` and a vocabulary file; the targets test of\n   490\t   §4.2.\n   491\t6. `frontends.cljc`: one `antlr-install` line.\n   492\t7. Optional: a probe (`scan` in cljc over `cst/first-failure`).\n   493\t\n   494\tSteps 2-6 are the same for every language; the generator, exporter, packet reader, REPL\n   495\tand catalog are not edited. A Java-only language coexists with Python on Node and Dart\n   496\tbuilds by construction (demonstrated in M2). TS and PHP gain a host the moment a helper\n   497\tfor that target is added to their manifests.\n   498\t\n   499\t## 8. Boundary (unchanged from v1)\n   500\t\n   501\tPer language: lowering, scope, prelude, publication, primitives, probe rules, refusal and\n   502\tdiagnostic namespaces, entry rule, trailing-newline normalization. Generic: generator,\n   503\t`packet.cljc`, the three `yang.antlr.cst` files, `yang.antlr.conformance`, `yang.frontend`.\n   504\tThe IR carries names only; no language-specific packet fields; more needs an IR version.\n   505\t\n   506\t## 9. Reconciliation with the TS and PHP designs (corrected, finding 9)\n   507\t\n   508\t- (a) Already covered: manifest and vendoring discipline (`yang.typescript.md` 5.1,\n   509\t  `yang.php.md` 6), the worker profile contract, per-target helper versioning\n   510\t  (`yang.antlr.md` 4.2), lifts L1-L4 (section 17). This plan edits none of them.\n   511\t- (b) New for Python on JS/Dart: multi-target generation, the exporters, the IR\n   512\t  formalization (§1), the conformance helper and goldens, the probe split, the gate\n   513\t  removal and availability mechanism, the pins and their enforcement.\n   514\t- (c) Generalizes: everything in (b) except Python's manifest rows and profile. For TS\n   515\t  the remaining per-target work is the helper port **plus** its dialect flag and\n   516\t  prediction-mode pins per target (`yang.typescript.md:504-518`); for PHP, the upstream\n   517\t  JavaScript base makes Node a helper-port problem and Dart a new-helper problem\n   518\t  (`yang.php.md:1319-1331`). \"Add the helper to the manifest\" is the mechanical part; the\n   519\t  helper itself is not.\n   520\t- Collision to sequence: L2 edits `packet.cljc` (D1); this plan edits `validate!` and adds\n   521\t  `conform`. Whichever lands second rebases; different functions.\n   522\t\n   523\t## 10. Legacy retirement (revised, finding 6)\n   524\t\n   525\tPreconditions, all demonstrable before the round opens:\n   526\t\n   527\t1. M2 landed with layers 1-3 green on three hosts and the comprehension line evaluating\n   528\t   on Node and Dart (R1).\n   529\t2. **Consumer inventory**: a fresh `grep -rn 'yang\\.python\\b'` over `src/` and `test/`,\n   530\t   excluding `yang.python.antlr`, with every hit dispositioned. Today's inventory:\n   531\t   `compilation_pipeline.cljs:50,158` (demo, `py/compile`); `frontends.cljc:15,68-69`\n   532\t   (legacy install); `repl.cljc:894` and `yin.repl.md` (docs naming `:yang.python/legacy`);\n   533\t   `src/cljc/yang/docs/{python,architecture,README}.md`; `test/yang/python_test.*` and\n   534\t   every test selecting `:yang.python/legacy` (for example\n   535\t   `python_antlr_test.cljc:329-334`); `subsystems.edn:50-51` (prefix stays, covers antlr).\n   536\t3. **Demo migration landed** (in M2 under §6.2, or as its own slice if the fallback is\n   537\t   chosen): the demo's `:python` branch parses through the ANTLR frontend and lowers\n   538\t   through `yang.python.antlr`; the engineer verifies the pipeline's AST consumer\n   539\t   (`vm/ast->datoms`) accepts the ANTLR lowering's output, or the demo takes checked-in\n   540\t   packets (PHP's B8.9 pattern).\n   541\t4. Every legacy test scenario ported or recorded as dropped; docs name the retirement.\n   542\t\n   543\tThe round then deletes `src/cljc/yang/python.cljc` and its tests, the `:yang.python/legacy`\n   544\tinstall, and amends the docs. Not part of the first engineer round.\n   545\t\n   546\t## 11. Implementation slices (resequenced; findings 4, 8)\n   547\t\n   548\t| Slice | Scope | Acceptance |\n   549\t|---|---|---|\n   550\t| M0 build | manifest `:targets` and structured `:runtime`; generator loop; JS and Dart generation with ported patches; digests; `gen:antlr` attached to `build:yin-repl-node`/`build:yin-repl-peer` and the JVM test tasks; both cljs classpaths; `package.json`+lock, `pubspec.yaml`+lock; pin-consistency check | Java digests byte-identical; JS/Dart artifacts generated, ASCII, digest-pinned; `bb gen:antlr` reproducible from clean; clean direct `bb build:yin-repl-node` and `bb build:yin-repl-peer` succeed; **executable smoke on Node and Dart**: a test namespace requires the generated recognizer and runtime, constructs lexer and parser, calls `file_input` on `\"x = 1\\n\"` and reads back `ruleNames`; `CharStreams.fromString` code-point mode confirmed; `pub get` resolves `antlr4 4.13.2` under SDK `>=3.7.0`; Dart dynamic-interop policy: the cljd compile log's dynamic-call warning count for the smoke namespace is zero (typed constructor and entry calls) |\n   551\t| M1 IR + exporters + profile | `validate!` rewrite (§1.2) and `conform` (§1.3) with the codex counterexamples as tests; `cst/vocabulary`; shared transform; `cst.cljs`, `cst.cljd` with instance-owned caches (§2.3); **`parser.cljc` for Python** (moved here from M2 so M1 has its own profile); `yang_antlr_goldens` and the committed goldens; `yang.antlr.conformance` with the interleaved-worker test; `cst_export_test` to cljc; probe split with portable strings (§2.4) | layer 1 green on JVM, Node, Dart: full-packet `=` to goldens including the UTF-8 fixture, indentation, both error fixtures under the §5.3 rules; vocabulary sets equal; interleaved-worker test green; `validate!` rejects both counterexamples and accepts error packets |\n   552\t| M2 catalog + REPL + browser | gate removal, `antlr-install`, derived `:yang.grammar/targets`, targets test, Java-only fixture language demonstration (§4.2); revision bump; layers 2-3 on all hosts; browser release build and bundle measurement, demo `:python` retarget (§6.2, §10.3); doc amendments (§6.1) | layers 2 and 3 green on three hosts; `(lang :yang.python/antlr)` evaluates the comprehension line at the Node and Dart prompts; Node and Dart answer `unavailable-parser` for the Java-only fixture while Python parses; `release demo` compiles, size delta recorded, decision A confirmed or fallback B applied; quick tiers green per host; kondo + cljstyle clean |\n   553\t| M3 legacy retirement | §10 | its own round, opens only with the §10 inventory dispositioned |\n   554\t\n   555\tLanes per slice: `bb test:clj` per iteration; full three-host quick tier at each landing,\n   556\tone lane set at a time, foreground; focused ANTLR namespaces with `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` at\n   557\tM2.\n   558\t\n   559\t## 12. Risks (v1 list, amended)\n   560\t\n   561\t- Index units (JS/Dart UTF-16 vs code points): the `é𝄞` fixture and `conform`'s boundary\n   562\t  check are the guards; highest-probability parity defect.\n   563\t- Dart helper untested upstream; bounded to one patched file; pub SDK bound verified in M0.\n   564\t- Circular ESM import between the JS lexer base and generated parser under shadow-cljs\n   565\t  classpath JS; verified by the M0 smoke (fallback `:js-options :resolve`).\n   566\t- Diagnostic wording across runtimes may resist normalization (§5.3): escalate, do not\n   567\t  relax.\n   568\t- Licence: four more helpers without notices; `:blocked-until` stays; pub licence recorded\n   569\t  in full.\n   570\t- Browser bundle growth (§6.2): measured, with a named fallback.\n   571\t- Generated static caches reachable past the worker on some runtime (§2.3): blocking M1\n   572\t  finding if found.\n   573\t- Generation needs network on first run; ClojureDart traps in the exporter walk.\n   574\t\n   575\t## Verification (end to end)\n   576\t\n   577\t1. `bb gen:antlr` from clean `build/` and `lib/antlr`; Java digests vs\n   578\t   `git show HEAD:antlr/python3/manifest.edn`; pin check passes.\n   579\t2. Clean direct `bb build:yin-repl-node`, `bb build:yin-repl-peer`; `clj -M:cljs-yin-repl`\n   580\t   starts.\n   581\t3. `bb test:clj`, `bb test:cljs`, `bb test:cljd` quick tiers, foreground, one at a time;\n   582\t   `Testing yang.antlr.conformance-test` and `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` appear in the\n   583\t   Node and Dart output.\n   584\t4. Node prompt and Dart peer: `(lang :yang.python/antlr)` then the comprehension line;\n   585\t   output equals the JVM's. The Java-only fixture answers `unavailable-parser` there.\n   586\t5. `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release demo`; size delta recorded.\n   587\t6. `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` focused ANTLR namespaces.\n   588\t```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '100,334p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings-v2.md | nl -ba -v100; cat src/cljc/yang/antlr/packet.cljc; cat src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '100,334p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings-v2.md | nl -ba -v100; cat src/cljc/yang/antlr/packet.cljc; cat src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj\"","aggregated_output":"   100\tantlr supported languages\"; \"maybe an antlr intermediate representation is needed if antlr\n   101\tdoesn't have that concept already.\"\n   102\t\n   103\tFacts (verified against the pinned sources; v1's list, two corrections marked):\n   104\t\n   105\t- grammars-v4 `python/python3` at `8af0d4c2` ships helper bases for Java, JavaScript,\n   106\t  Dart, C#, C++, Go, Python3, TypeScript; `desc.xml` tests `Cpp;CSharp;Java;JavaScript;Python3`.\n   107\t  The Dart helper is not exercised upstream.\n   108\t- The JS and Dart lexer bases carry the same NEWLINE/DEDENT `commonToken` construction the\n   109\t  Java patch fixes; the patch is ported per target.\n   110\t- JS helpers: ESM, `import antlr4 from 'antlr4'`, circular import with the generated parser.\n   111\t  Dart helpers: `package:antlr4/antlr4.dart`, `dart:io`. No licence notice in either.\n   112\t- Runtimes: npm `antlr4` 4.13.2 (BSD-3-Clause, ESM, node>=16); pub `antlr4` 4.13.2 (SDK\n   113\t  `>=2.12.0 <3.0.0`, deps `logging`, `collection`). **Corrected (finding 9):** the pub\n   114\t  package's published licence combines BSD-3-Clause with MIT notices for bundled parts; the\n   115\t  manifest records the full published text at pin time, not \"BSD-3-Clause\".\n   116\t- ANTLR has no target-neutral tree; the repo's neutral form is CST packet v1\n   117\t  (`cst.clj:16-35`), read through `packet.cljc`. **Corrected (finding 2):** the hand-built\n   118\t  packets of `lower_portable_test.cljc` and `python_antlr_test.cljc` are lowering fixtures\n   119\t  (zero spans, no synthetic flags, grammar `\"hand\"`), not parser goldens.\n   120\t- TS (`yang.typescript.md` 5.3) and PHP (`yang.php.md` 6) are JVM-only for helper\n   121\t  availability reasons: TS has Java/C#/C++ bases upstream (plus dialect-flag and\n   122\t  prediction-mode requirements of its own); PHP has Java (vendored, MIT), JavaScript,\n   123\t  C#, C++, Python3, Antlr4ng bases and no Dart one.\n   124\t\n   125\t## The design in one paragraph (unchanged)\n   126\t\n   127\tANTLR gives \"one grammar, N generated recognizers\". The repo adds the other half: one\n   128\ttarget-neutral parse-tree IR that every host's exporter produces and every language's\n   129\tlowering consumes. That IR is CST packet v1, now formally owned by `packet.cljc` with an\n   130\toutcome-specific validator and a conformance oracle. A language is: a manifest (grammar,\n   131\tper-target helpers, patches, digests, runtime pins), a ~40-line `parser.cljc` with one\n   132\treader-conditional branch per generated target, its lowering over the IR, one catalog\n   133\t`install` line, and its goldens. Nothing per language touches the generator, the exporter,\n   134\tthe packet reader, or the REPL.\n   135\t\n   136\t## 1. The IR: `:yang.cst/v1`, formalized\n   137\t\n   138\t### 1.1 Home and ruling (unchanged)\n   139\t\n   140\tThe IR is the existing packet shape, not a new schema. `packet.cljc` owns it: the shape\n   141\tdoc at `cst.clj:16-35` moves there verbatim; `packet-version` moves there. D1 (the\n   142\tdiagnostic namespace) stays with lift L2 of `yang.typescript.md` 17.\n   143\t\n   144\t### 1.2 Structural validation is outcome-specific (revised, finding 1)\n   145\t\n   146\t`packet/validate!` is rewritten; its contract is per outcome:\n   147\t\n   148\t- Every outcome: `:yang.cst/version 1`, `:yang.cst/outcome` in\n   149\t  `#{:yang.cst/ok :yang.cst/syntax-error :yang.cst/source-error}`, `:yang.cst/complete true`,\n   150\t  `:yang.cst/grammar` a string, `:yang.cst/unit` present. Unknown outcome: malformed.\n   151\t- `:yang.cst/ok`: `:yang.cst/root 0`; `:yang.cst/nodes` a non-empty vector; no\n   152\t  `:yang.cst/errors`. **Preorder, uniqueness and reachability are established by one\n   153\t  recursive walk** from node 0 with a counter: visiting node `i` requires `(:id (nth nodes\n   154\t  counter)) = counter = i`, then walks its `:children` in order, each child's id being the\n   155\t  counter at the moment it is visited; after the walk the counter equals `(count nodes)`.\n   156\t  This rejects duplicate edges (the second visit finds the counter past the id), the\n   157\t  index-ordered non-preorder tree (`[0 1 3 2]`: node 1's child must be 2), forward\n   158\t  references, and unreachable nodes, in one pass. A rule node's `:children` is a vector\n   159\t  (possibly empty); a token node has no `:children`. `:kind` in `#{:rule :token}`; rule\n   160\t  nodes carry a string `:rule`, token nodes a string `:type` and string `:text`; every node\n   161\t  a `[s e]` span of two non-negative integers with `s <= e`.\n   162\t- `:yang.cst/syntax-error` and `:yang.cst/source-error`: no `:yang.cst/nodes`, no\n   163\t  `:yang.cst/root`; `:yang.cst/errors` a non-empty vector of records whose `:kind` is\n   164\t  `:syntax-error` (line >= 1, column >= 0, span nil or `[s e]`) or `:source-error`, each with\n   165\t  a string `:message`.\n   166\t\n   167\tA legitimate error packet therefore validates. The tests `validate!` gains: the two codex\n   168\tcounterexamples, an error packet, a token with children, a rule without `:children`.\n   169\t\n   170\t### 1.3 `conform`: content against source and vocabulary (revised, finding 1)\n   171\t\n   172\t`packet/conform` takes `packet`, the sealed `source` string, and a `vocabulary`\n   173\t`{:rule-names #{...} :token-names #{...}}`. It runs after `validate!`. Returns the packet\n   174\tor throws a qualified `malformed-cst` naming the first violated invariant. Checks, for\n   175\t`:yang.cst/ok`:\n   176\t\n   177\t1. **Names**: every `:rule` is in `:rule-names`; every `:type` is in `:token-names` or is\n   178\t   `\"EOF\"`. The vocabulary is exported by each host (`cst/vocabulary worker`, §2.1), so the\n   179\t   check is against the generated tables of the running host; cross-host name-set\n   180\t   equality is a separate golden (§5.1).\n   181\t2. **Byte bounds and UTF-8 boundaries**: let `B` be the set of byte offsets that start a\n   182\t   code point in `source`, plus `utf8-length`. Every span end is in `B`. (Portable: build\n   183\t   `B` by walking code points with `utf8-length` as `cst.clj:68-88` does; no host string\n   184\t   internals.)\n   185\t3. **Real-token text**: for a token without `:synthetic`, `s < e` and the source bytes at\n   186\t   `[s e]` decode to exactly `:text` (decode via the code-point index that `B` gives;\n   187\t   compare strings).\n   188\t4. **Terminal ordering** (stated separately from rule spans): walking tokens in preorder,\n   189\t   each real token's `s` is >= the previous real token's `e`; a synthetic token has `s = e`\n   190\t   and sits at an offset >= the previous real token's `e` and <= the next real token's `s`.\n   191\t5. **Rule extents over real descendants**: a rule's span equals `[min s, max e]` over the\n   192\t   real tokens in its subtree; a rule with no real descendant is zero-width at an offset\n   193\t   between its neighbouring real tokens (rule 4 applied to the empty rule's position).\n   194\t6. **Synthetic flag is justified**: a token marked `:synthetic` either has text different\n   195\t   from the source at its position or would overlap the previous real token; a token not\n   196\t   marked has text equal to the source at its span (this is check 3 from the other side,\n   197\t   so an exporter cannot hide a misplaced token behind the flag).\n   198\t\n   199\tFor error outcomes: each `:syntax-error` span, when present, satisfies 2; `:line`/`:column`\n   200\tare consistent with the span (the line containing byte `s` and the code-point column within\n   201\tit, computed from `source`). For `:source-error`: nothing beyond `validate!`.\n   202\t\n   203\tNot checked by `conform`: that the root's end equals `utf8-length` (false when the source\n   204\tends in untokenized trivia); fixtures assert it where true.\n   205\t\n   206\t`conform` costs nothing at runtime (tests only).\n   207\t\n   208\t### 1.4 Normative invariants (restated; they are what §1.2-1.3 check)\n   209\t\n   210\t1. Ids are DFS-preorder indexes, root 0, children a tree (§1.2 walk).\n   211\t2. `:rule` and `:type` come from the host's generated tables (§1.3.1); `\"EOF\"` for EOF.\n   212\t3. Spans are half-open UTF-8 byte offsets on code-point boundaries (§1.3.2); each target\n   213\t   converts its own index unit at the boundary.\n   214\t4. A real token's text is the source at its span; a synthetic token is zero-width (§1.3.3, 6).\n   215\t5. Terminals are ordered and non-overlapping; rule spans cover real descendants (§1.3.4, 5).\n   216\t6. A syntax error yields an error packet with errors and no nodes (§1.2).\n   217\t7. `:yang.cst/grammar` names grammar revision and tool; the target does not appear in the\n   218\t   packet, in node fields, or in diagnostic text (§5.3).\n   219\t\n   220\t## 2. The exporter: `yang.antlr.cst` on three hosts\n   221\t\n   222\t### 2.1 Contract (v1 plus one export)\n   223\t\n   224\t`cst.clj` stays. `cst.cljs` and `cst.cljd` implement the same public names: `make-worker`,\n   225\t`parse-unit`, `parse-transform`, `utf8-bytes`, `first-failure` (§2.4), and **`vocabulary`**\n   226\t(new): `{:rule-names #{...} :token-names #{...}}` from the worker's parser (`ruleNames`,\n   227\tsymbolic and literal names). Profile contract unchanged:\n   228\t`{:grammar-id :make-lexer :make-parser :start}`.\n   229\t\n   230\t`parse-transform` and `source-error` are host-free and move to a cljc helper (engineer's\n   231\tcall on the namespace; minimal diff), so each host file holds only the host walk.\n   232\t\n   233\t### 2.2 Host walks (unchanged from v1)\n   234\t\n   235\tNode: `CharStreams.fromString` (code points), `CommonTokenStream`, an `ErrorListener`\n   236\tsubclass, `ctx.children`, `ctx.ruleIndex`, `parser.ruleNames`, `symbolicNames`/`literalNames`,\n   237\t`node.symbol.{type,start,stop,text}`. Dart: `InputStream.fromString`, `CommonTokenStream`,\n   238\t`BaseErrorListener`, `childCount/getChild`, `TerminalNode.symbol`, `ruleNames`, `vocabulary`.\n   239\tRecorded ClojureDart traps apply (reader-conditional ordering, `for` over chunked seqs,\n   240\tmulti-key `assoc` on nil, unary minus, protocol `[_ _]`).\n   241\t\n   242\t### 2.3 Worker isolation: the mechanism per runtime, no fallback (revised, finding 5)\n   243\t\n   244\tThe v1 fallback (\"document that the host is single-threaded and the cache is read-mostly\")\n   245\tis removed. `yang.antlr.md` §4.3 requires isolation; both runtimes support the JVM's\n   246\tmechanism exactly:\n   247\t\n   248\t| Runtime | Instance-owned caches | How the worker installs them |\n   249\t|---|---|---|\n   250\t| JVM (today) | `DFA[]` per decision, `PredictionContextCache` | `setInterpreter` with `LexerATNSimulator`/`ParserATNSimulator` (`cst.clj:242-258`) |\n   251\t| antlr4 npm 4.13.2 | `DFA` array, `PredictionContextCache` exported | `lexer._interp = new LexerATNSimulator(lexer, atn, decisionToDFA, cache)`, same for the parser's `_interp` |\n   252\t| antlr4 pub 4.13.2 | `DFA`, `PredictionContextCache` | `lexer.interpreter = LexerATNSimulator(lexer, atn, decisionToDFA, cache)`, same for the parser |\n   253\t\n   254\t`make-worker` on each host builds fresh DFA arrays and caches from the recognizer's ATN\n   255\t(as `cst.clj:100-124`); `parse-unit` installs them on every fresh lexer and parser. The\n   256\tgenerated recognizers' static shared caches are never used by a worker's parse.\n   257\t\n   258\tVerification (in `yang.antlr.conformance`, every host): an **interleaved-worker test**:\n   259\tworkers `w1`, `w2`; parse A on `w1`, B on `w2`, A' (a variant of A that exercises other\n   260\tdecisions) on `w1`, then A on `w2`; every packet equals the packet a fresh worker gives\n   261\tfor the same source; `w1`'s and `w2`'s DFA arrays and caches are distinct objects\n   262\t(`identical?` false); and on the JVM, Node and Dart alike the recognizer's static\n   263\t`_decisionToDFA`/`_sharedContextCache` is not the object installed on the worker. If a\n   264\truntime's generated code reaches its static cache through a path the worker cannot\n   265\tredirect, that is a blocking M1 finding, not a documented exception.\n   266\t\n   267\t### 2.4 Probe (revised, finding 8)\n   268\t\n   269\t`scan`/`probe` move to `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/repl_probe.cljc` **with portable string\n   270\taccess**: `(nth text i)` for a character, `subs` plus `=` (or `clojure.string/starts-with?`\n   271\ton a `subs`) for the closer test, `count` for length; no `.charAt`, `.startsWith`,\n   272\t`.offsetByCodePoints`. The host-specific `first-failure` becomes a `yang.antlr.cst` export\n   273\t(`first-failure worker source` answering `nil | :end | :early`) per host. The probe's\n   274\texisting JVM tests become cljc and run on three hosts (a scan-golden table is portable by\n   275\tconstruction).\n   276\t\n   277\t## 3. The build: one manifest, N targets\n   278\t\n   279\t### 3.1 Manifest (v1 shape; `:runtime` becomes structured, finding 9)\n   280\t\n   281\tAs v1, with these corrections:\n   282\t\n   283\t```clojure\n   284\t:targets\n   285\t{:java       {:runtime {:maven \"org.antlr/antlr4-runtime\" :version \"4.13.2\"} ...}\n   286\t :javascript {:runtime {:npm \"antlr4\" :version \"4.13.2\"} ...}\n   287\t :dart       {:runtime {:pub \"antlr4\" :version \"4.13.2\"} ...}}\n   288\t:license {...  \"antlr4 4.13.2 (npm)\" \"BSD-3-Clause (LICENSE.txt at pin)\"\n   289\t          \"antlr4 4.13.2 (pub)\" \"<full published licence text summary: BSD-3-Clause plus the MIT notices it carries>\"\n   290\t          \"JavaScript/Python3LexerBase.js\" \"no notice; UNRESOLVED\" ...}\n   291\t```\n   292\t\n   293\tHelper files are fetched by digest into `build/antlr/<grammar>/upstream` as today. The\n   294\t\"vendor `.g4` only\" rule has one recorded exception: a helper with an explicit licence\n   295\tnotice may be vendored beside the `.g4` with its notice (PHP's `Java/PhpLexerBase.java`,\n   296\t`yang.php.md:1319-1325`); the manifest's `:files` entry says `:vendored` either way, as\n   297\ttoday's generator already supports.\n   298\t\n   299\t### 3.2 Patches, digests, landing dirs (unchanged from v1)\n   300\t\n   301\tPer-target `:patches` (positioned-token port for JS and Dart), per-target `:generated`\n   302\tdigests and `generated-digests.edn`, ASCII check per target. Java byte-identical. Node:\n   303\t`build/antlr/python3/js` with ESM under `yang/antlr/gen/python3/`; Dart: `lib/antlr/python3`\n   304\t(`lib/` is gitignored, package root `datomworld_cljd`).\n   305\t\n   306\t### 3.3 Generator loop (unchanged)\n   307\t\n   308\t`yang_antlr_gen.clj` loops over `:targets`; `-Dlanguage=` is the per-target switch; the\n   309\tpackage-clause prepend and `javac` stay Java-only.\n   310\t\n   311\t### 3.4 Build ordering and classpaths (revised, finding 4)\n   312\t\n   313\t- `bb.edn`: `gen:python-antlr` becomes `gen:antlr` (all grammars, all targets,\n   314\t  `--grammar`/`--target` filters). **`:depends [gen:antlr]` is attached to the consuming\n   315\t  build tasks**: `build:yin-repl-node` and `build:yin-repl-peer`. `test:clj`,\n   316\t  `test:all:clj`, `test:slow:clj` keep a direct dependency too (the JVM lane loads classes\n   317\t  without a build task). The `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` families inherit through the builds.\n   318\t  Every remaining `gen:python-antlr` reference (docs, `build-n-test.md`) is renamed.\n   319\t- `deps.edn`: `build/antlr/python3/js` is added to **both** `:cljs` and `:cljs-yin-repl`\n   320\t  `:extra-paths` (`deps.edn:39,67-72`); the `:dev` alias too, since `watch demo` runs\n   321\t  through it. Dart needs no path: generated files are package sources.\n   322\t- Acceptance is a **clean direct build**, not a test wrapper: from a clean `build/` and\n   323\t  `lib/antlr`, `bb build:yin-repl-node` and `bb build:yin-repl-peer` each succeed\n   324\t  on their own, and `clj -M:cljs-yin-repl` starts.\n   325\t\n   326\t### 3.5 Pins, lockfiles, consistency (revised, finding 9)\n   327\t\n   328\t- `package.json` gains `\"antlr4\": \"4.13.2\"` (exact) and `package-lock.json` is updated in\n   329\t  the same commit; `pubspec.yaml` gains `antlr4: 4.13.2` and `pubspec.lock` likewise.\n   330\t- `gen:antlr` **enforces** that for every target in the manifest the runtime pin equals\n   331\t  the installed/locked version: Maven via the `:antlr-gen` classpath (as today), npm via\n   332\t  `package-lock.json`'s resolved `antlr4` version, pub via `pubspec.lock`. A mismatch fails\n   333\t  generation with the three versions printed. Tool and runtimes remain one release.\n   334\t\n(ns yang.antlr.packet\n  \"Reading a portable CST packet (docs/design/yang.antlr.md §5.5; the shape\n   is documented on `yang.antlr.cst`). Portable and language-agnostic:\n   child edges are data, so the tree is validated before anything walks\n   it.\")\n\n\n(defn- malformed!\n  [message data]\n  (throw (ex-info message\n                  (merge {:yang.python.antlr/diagnostic\n                          :yang.python.antlr/malformed-cst}\n                         data))))\n\n\n(defn validate!\n  \"Return the packet when its nodes form one tree rooted at 0 with preorder\n   ids equal to their index and every child referenced exactly once, after\n   its parent; otherwise throw a qualified malformed-cst error.\"\n  [packet]\n  (let [nodes (:yang.cst/nodes packet)\n        n (count nodes)]\n    (when-not (and (vector? nodes) (pos? n) (= 0 (:yang.cst/root packet)))\n      (malformed! \"CST packet has no root\" {}))\n    (loop [i 0\n           seen #{}]\n      (if (< i n)\n        (let [node (nth nodes i)\n              kids (:children node)]\n          (when-not (= i (:id node))\n            (malformed! \"CST node id is not its index\" {:index i}))\n          (when-not (contains? #{:rule :token} (:kind node))\n            (malformed! \"CST node has an unknown kind\" {:id i}))\n          (doseq [k kids]\n            (when-not (and (integer? k) (< i k n) (not (contains? seen k)))\n              (malformed! \"CST child edge is not a tree edge\"\n                          {:parent i, :child k})))\n          (recur (inc i) (into seen kids)))\n        (when-not (= (dec n) (count seen))\n          (malformed! \"CST has nodes unreachable from the root\"\n                      {:reachable (count seen), :nodes n}))))\n    packet))\n\n\n(defn node\n  [packet id]\n  (nth (:yang.cst/nodes packet) id))\n\n\n(defn root\n  [packet]\n  (node packet (:yang.cst/root packet)))\n\n\n(defn children\n  [packet n]\n  (mapv #(node packet %) (:children n)))\n\n\n(defn rule?\n  ([n] (= :rule (:kind n)))\n  ([n rule-name] (and (= :rule (:kind n)) (= rule-name (:rule n)))))\n\n\n(defn token?\n  ([n] (= :token (:kind n)))\n  ([n text] (and (= :token (:kind n)) (= text (:text n)))))\n\n\n(defn child-rules\n  \"The rule children of `n` named `rule-name`, in order.\"\n  [packet n rule-name]\n  (filterv #(rule? % rule-name) (children packet n)))\n\n\n(defn has-token?\n  [packet n text]\n  (boolean (some #(token? % text) (children packet n))))\n(ns yang.antlr.cst\n  \"The host parser interpreter's generic half (docs/design/yang.antlr.md §4.3,\n   §5.1, §5.5): assemble sealed source units, parse each with an ANTLR\n   grammar profile, and export one portable CST packet per unit.\n\n   Nothing here knows a language. A grammar profile supplies the host\n   constructors:\n\n     {:grammar-id  string naming grammar revision and tool\n      :make-lexer  (fn [CharStream] Lexer)\n      :make-parser (fn [TokenStream] Parser)\n      :start       (fn [Parser] ParserRuleContext)}\n\n   No ANTLR object leaves this namespace: a packet is plain data.\n\n   Packet (version 1):\n\n     {:yang.cst/version 1\n      :yang.cst/unit u\n      :yang.cst/grammar id\n      :yang.cst/outcome :yang.cst/ok | :yang.cst/syntax-error | :yang.cst/source-error\n      :yang.cst/root 0                       ; ok only\n      :yang.cst/nodes [record ...]           ; ok only, preorder, id = index\n      :yang.cst/errors [error ...]           ; non-ok only\n      :yang.cst/complete true}\n\n   Rule record  {:id n :kind :rule :rule \\\"name\\\" :children [ids] :span [s e]}\n   Token record {:id n :kind :token :type \\\"NAME\\\" :text t :span [s e]}\n                 plus :synthetic true when the token's text is not the source\n                 at its span (indentation helpers, EOF); its span is then\n                 zero-width.\n   Error record {:kind :syntax-error :message m :line l :column c :span [s e]}\n\n   Spans are half-open UTF-8 byte offsets into the sealed source. A unit with\n   any syntax error yields an error packet and no nodes: never a partial\n   program.\"\n  (:import\n    (java.nio.charset\n      StandardCharsets)\n    (org.antlr.v4.runtime\n      BaseErrorListener\n      CharStreams\n      CommonTokenStream\n      Lexer\n      Parser\n      ParserRuleContext\n      Recognizer\n      Token)\n    (org.antlr.v4.runtime.atn\n      ATN\n      LexerATNSimulator\n      ParserATNSimulator\n      PredictionContextCache)\n    (org.antlr.v4.runtime.dfa\n      DFA)\n    (org.antlr.v4.runtime.tree\n      ParseTree\n      TerminalNode)))\n\n\n(def packet-version 1)\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Source coordinates\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- utf8-length\n  [cp]\n  (cond (< cp 0x80) 1\n        (< cp 0x800) 2\n        (< cp 0x10000) 3\n        :else 4))\n\n\n(defn- byte-offsets\n  \"Byte offset of each code point index of `s`, plus the total at index n.\n   ANTLR's CodePointCharStream indexes code points.\"\n  ^longs [^String s]\n  (let [cps (.toArray (.codePoints s))\n        n (alength cps)\n        out (long-array (inc n))]\n    (loop [i 0\n           acc 0]\n      (aset out i acc)\n      (when (< i n)\n        (recur (inc i) (+ acc (utf8-length (aget cps i))))))\n    out))\n\n\n(defn- offset-at\n  [^longs offsets i]\n  (aget offsets (max 0 (min i (dec (alength offsets))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Worker: exclusively owned recognizer caches (§4.3)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- fresh-dfa\n  ^\"[Lorg.antlr.v4.runtime.dfa.DFA;\" [^ATN atn]\n  (into-array DFA\n              (map (fn [i] (DFA. (.getDecisionState atn i) i))\n                   (range (.getNumberOfDecisions atn)))))\n\n\n(defn make-worker\n  \"A host worker for `profile` owning its own DFA and prediction-context\n   caches, so no parse shares mutable recognizer state with another worker.\n   The worker is host state owned by one parser stage; it is not data and\n   never crosses a stream.\"\n  [profile]\n  (let [lexer ^Lexer ((:make-lexer profile) (CharStreams/fromString \"\"))\n        parser ^Parser ((:make-parser profile)\n                        (CommonTokenStream. lexer))\n        lexer-atn (.getATN lexer)\n        parser-atn (.getATN parser)]\n    {:profile profile,\n     :lexer-atn lexer-atn,\n     :lexer-dfa (fresh-dfa lexer-atn),\n     :lexer-cache (PredictionContextCache.),\n     :parser-atn parser-atn,\n     :parser-dfa (fresh-dfa parser-atn),\n     :parser-cache (PredictionContextCache.)}))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Export\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- error-listener\n  \"Collects syntax errors as data into `errors` (a volatile).\"\n  [errors offsets]\n  (proxy [BaseErrorListener] []\n    (syntaxError\n      [^Recognizer recognizer offending line column msg _e]\n      (let [span (if (instance? Token offending)\n                   (let [^Token t offending\n                         s (offset-at offsets (.getStartIndex t))\n                         e (if (< (.getStopIndex t) (.getStartIndex t))\n                             s\n                             (offset-at offsets (inc (.getStopIndex t))))]\n                     [s e])\n                   (if (instance? Lexer recognizer)\n                     (let [^Lexer lx recognizer]\n                       [(offset-at offsets (._tokenStartCharIndex lx))\n                        (offset-at offsets (.index (.getInputStream lx)))])\n                     nil))]\n        (vswap! errors conj\n                {:kind :syntax-error,\n                 :message (str msg),\n                 :line line,\n                 :column column,\n                 :span span})))))\n\n\n(defn- token-record\n  \"`last-stop` is the stop index of the previous real token: a token that\n   overlaps it was produced by a helper, not read from the source (the\n   Python helper's DEDENT reports the newline it follows as its text).\"\n  [^Parser parser ^String source ^longs offsets last-stop id ^Token t]\n  (let [type (.getType t)\n        vocab (.getVocabulary parser)\n        type-name (if (= type Token/EOF)\n                    \"EOF\"\n                    (or (.getSymbolicName vocab type)\n                        (.getLiteralName vocab type)\n                        (str type)))\n        start (.getStartIndex t)\n        stop (.getStopIndex t)\n        text (.getText t)\n        n (dec (alength offsets))\n        real-text (when (and (<= 0 start) (<= start stop) (< stop n))\n                    (let [s (.offsetByCodePoints source 0 start)\n                          e (.offsetByCodePoints source 0 (inc stop))]\n                      (subs source s e)))\n        synthetic? (or (not= real-text text) (<= start last-stop))\n        span (if synthetic?\n               (let [p (offset-at offsets (inc stop))] [p p])\n               [(offset-at offsets start) (offset-at offsets (inc stop))])]\n    (cond-> {:id id, :kind :token, :type type-name, :text text, :span span}\n      synthetic? (assoc :synthetic true))))\n\n\n(defn- export-nodes\n  \"Preorder records for the parse tree; a record's id is its index.\"\n  [^Parser parser source offsets ^ParseTree root]\n  (let [rule-names (.getRuleNames parser)\n        out (volatile! [])\n        last-stop (volatile! -1)]\n    (letfn [(walk\n              [^ParseTree t]\n              (let [id (count @out)]\n                (if (instance? TerminalNode t)\n                  (let [tok (.getSymbol ^TerminalNode t)\n                        record (token-record parser source offsets @last-stop\n                                             id tok)]\n                    (when-not (:synthetic record)\n                      (vreset! last-stop (.getStopIndex tok)))\n                    (vswap! out conj record))\n                  (let [^ParserRuleContext ctx t]\n                    (vswap! out conj nil)\n                    (let [kids (mapv #(walk (.getChild ctx (int %)))\n                                     (range (.getChildCount ctx)))\n                          records @out\n                          ;; a rule covers its real text: synthetic tokens\n                          ;; and empty rules do not stretch it\n                          child-spans (keep (fn [k]\n                                              (let [r (nth records k)\n                                                    [a b] (:span r)]\n                                                (when (and (not (:synthetic r))\n                                                           (< a b))\n                                                  [a b])))\n                                            kids)\n                          start-tok (.getStart ctx)\n                          s (if start-tok\n                              (offset-at offsets (.getStartIndex start-tok))\n                              0)\n                          span (if (seq child-spans)\n                                 [(apply min (map first child-spans))\n                                  (apply max (map second child-spans))]\n                                 [s s])]\n                      (vswap! out assoc id\n                              {:id id,\n                               :kind :rule,\n                               :rule (aget rule-names (.getRuleIndex ctx)),\n                               :children kids,\n                               :span span}))))\n                id))]\n      (walk root)\n      @out)))\n\n\n(defn parse-unit\n  \"Parse one sealed unit's `source` with `worker`; return its CST packet.\"\n  [worker unit ^String source]\n  (let [{:keys [profile]} worker\n        offsets (byte-offsets source)\n        errors (volatile! [])\n        listener (error-listener errors offsets)\n        lexer ^Lexer ((:make-lexer profile) (CharStreams/fromString source))\n        _ (.setInterpreter lexer\n                           (LexerATNSimulator. lexer\n                                               ^ATN (:lexer-atn worker)\n                                               ^\"[Lorg.antlr.v4.runtime.dfa.DFA;\"\n                                               (:lexer-dfa worker)\n                                               ^PredictionContextCache\n                                               (:lexer-cache worker)))\n        _ (.removeErrorListeners lexer)\n        _ (.addErrorListener lexer listener)\n        parser ^Parser ((:make-parser profile) (CommonTokenStream. lexer))\n        _ (.setInterpreter parser\n                           (ParserATNSimulator. parser\n                                                ^ATN (:parser-atn worker)\n                                                ^\"[Lorg.antlr.v4.runtime.dfa.DFA;\"\n                                                (:parser-dfa worker)\n                                                ^PredictionContextCache\n                                                (:parser-cache worker)))\n        _ (.removeErrorListeners parser)\n        _ (.addErrorListener parser listener)\n        tree ((:start profile) parser)\n        base {:yang.cst/version packet-version,\n              :yang.cst/unit unit,\n              :yang.cst/grammar (:grammar-id profile),\n              :yang.cst/complete true}]\n    (if (seq @errors)\n      (assoc base\n             :yang.cst/outcome :yang.cst/syntax-error\n             :yang.cst/errors @errors)\n      (assoc base\n             :yang.cst/outcome :yang.cst/ok\n             :yang.cst/root 0\n             :yang.cst/nodes (export-nodes parser source offsets tree)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Source units (§5.1) and the parser stage transform\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- source-error\n  [grammar-id unit message data]\n  {:yang.cst/version packet-version,\n   :yang.cst/unit unit,\n   :yang.cst/grammar grammar-id,\n   :yang.cst/outcome :yang.cst/source-error,\n   :yang.cst/errors [(merge {:kind :source-error, :message message} data)],\n   :yang.cst/complete true})\n\n\n(defn parse-transform\n  \"The parser stage's transform over source events: chunks accumulate per\n   unit by ordinal; a seal with every chunk present assembles the snapshot,\n   parses it, and emits one packet on port `:cst`. A conflicting duplicate\n   chunk, a seal with chunks missing, or an unknown event emits a\n   source-error packet instead. State is `{unit {ordinal text}}`.\"\n  [worker]\n  (let [grammar-id (get-in worker [:profile :grammar-id])]\n    (fn [units event]\n      (let [unit (:yang.source/unit event)]\n        (case (:yang.source/event event)\n          :yang.source/chunk\n          (let [ordinal (:yang.source/ordinal event)\n                text (:yang.source/text event)\n                prior (get-in units [unit ordinal])]\n            (if (and (some? prior) (not= prior text))\n              [(dissoc units unit)\n               [[:cst (source-error grammar-id unit \"Conflicting duplicate chunk\"\n                                    {:ordinal ordinal})]]]\n              [(assoc-in units [unit ordinal] text) []]))\n\n          :yang.source/seal\n          (let [chunks (get units unit {})\n                n (:yang.source/chunk-count event)]\n            (if (= (set (range n)) (set (keys chunks)))\n              [(dissoc units unit)\n               [[:cst (parse-unit worker unit\n                                  (apply str (map chunks (range n))))]]]\n              [(dissoc units unit)\n               [[:cst (source-error grammar-id unit \"Seal with missing chunks\"\n                                    {:chunk-count n,\n                                     :present (vec (sort (keys chunks)))})]]]))\n\n          [units\n           [[:cst (source-error grammar-id unit \"Unknown source event\"\n                                {:event (:yang.source/event event)})]]])))))\n\n\n(defn utf8-bytes\n  \"The UTF-8 byte count of a string: span ends are checked against it.\"\n  [^String s]\n  (alength (.getBytes s StandardCharsets/UTF_8)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The revisions address the missing-target imports, build ordering, genuine goldens, and retirement prerequisites. I’m checking two remaining contract risks: whether synthetic-token validation can detect mislabelled real tokens, and whether error spans always identify the location reported by ANTLR’s listener."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat src/clj/yang/python/antlr/repl_probe.clj; cat test/yang/antlr/cst_export_test.clj; cat bb.edn; cat deps.edn; sed -n '1,150p' src/cljc/yang/frontend.cljc; sed -n '1670,1715p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; cat antlr/python3/manifest.edn\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat src/clj/yang/python/antlr/repl_probe.clj; cat test/yang/antlr/cst_export_test.clj; cat bb.edn; cat deps.edn; sed -n '1,150p' src/cljc/yang/frontend.cljc; sed -n '1670,1715p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; cat antlr/python3/manifest.edn\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yang.python.antlr.repl-probe\n  \"The Python ANTLR frontend's completeness probe (docs/design/yang.antlr.md\n   section 8.5.6, slice F3; JVM only, as the parser is). `probe` answers\n   whether the text typed so far is a whole submission:\n\n   - `:complete`: the text parses, or it is wrong in a way more lines cannot\n     mend (the shell then reports the syntax error);\n   - `:incomplete`: more lines can still finish it.\n\n   Two passes decide. A lexical `scan` follows short and triple-quoted\n   strings with their escapes, comments, typed bracket nesting and explicit\n   backslash continuation: a string still open waits for more lines unless\n   the text, with that string standing as an empty one, already fails on\n   or before it; a bracket or continuation still open waits too (a blank\n   line among them is legal Python), and a closer that matches no opener\n   is an error at once. Then\n   the parser runs, and its first error is classified by the token it\n   failed on: only a failure on the end-of-input tokens (EOF, and the\n   NEWLINE and DEDENT the lexer synthesizes there) is mendable by more\n   lines; any earlier token is not, so the error is reported even under an\n   open bracket. With brackets, strings and continuations closed, a blank\n   line ends the submission, and text that parses waits only while its\n   last logical line is indented: as in CPython's REPL, a blank line closes\n   a block.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [yang.python.antlr.parser :as parser])\n  (:import\n    (org.antlr.v4.runtime\n      ANTLRErrorListener\n      BaseErrorListener\n      CharStreams\n      CommonTokenStream\n      Lexer\n      NoViableAltException\n      Parser\n      Token)\n    (org.antlr.v4.runtime.atn\n      ATN\n      LexerATNSimulator\n      ParserATNSimulator\n      PredictionContextCache)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lexical scan\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private opener-of\n  {\\) \\(, \\] \\[, \\} \\{})\n\n\n(defn- string-end\n  \"The index just past the string literal whose body starts at `i`, quoted\n   by `q`, tripled when `triple?`: `:open` when more lines can still close\n   it (a triple string, or a short string whose line ends in a backslash),\n   `:broken` when a short string's line ends without one. A backslash\n   escapes the next character, a newline included, in raw strings too.\"\n  [^String text i q triple?]\n  (let [n (count text)\n        closer (if triple? (str q q q) (str q))]\n    (loop [i i]\n      (if (>= i n)\n        (if triple? :open :broken)\n        (let [c (.charAt text i)]\n          (cond\n            (= \\\\ c) (if (< (inc i) n) (recur (+ i 2)) :open)\n            (.startsWith text closer (int i)) (+ i (count closer))\n            (and (not triple?) (= \\newline c)) :broken\n            :else (recur (inc i))))))))\n\n\n(defn scan\n  \"The lexical state at the end of `text`:\n\n     {:brackets [opener ...]   ; still open, innermost last\n      :block? b}               ; the last logical line is indented\n\n   plus `:string :open` when the text ends inside a string (`:string-at`\n   its opening quote), `:continued\n   true` when it ends on an explicit backslash continuation, or `:error`\n   (`:bracket`, `:string`) for a closer with no matching opener or a short\n   string broken by a newline; the scan stops there.\"\n  [^String text]\n  (let [n (count text)]\n    ;; line-begin: where the current logical line's physical line began;\n    ;; line?: no code yet on the current logical line\n    (loop [i 0\n           stack []\n           line-begin 0\n           line? true\n           block? false]\n      (let [state {:brackets stack, :block? block?}\n            code (fn [stack]\n                   [stack (if line? (> i line-begin) block?)])]\n        (if (>= i n)\n          state\n          (let [c (.charAt text i)]\n            (cond\n              (= \\# c)\n              (let [eol (str/index-of text \"\\n\" i)]\n                (recur (or eol n) stack line-begin line? block?))\n\n              (= \\newline c)\n              (if (empty? stack)\n                (recur (inc i) stack (inc i) true block?)\n                (recur (inc i) stack line-begin line? block?))\n\n              (or (= \\\" c) (= \\' c))\n              (let [triple? (.startsWith text (str c c c) (int i))\n                    end (string-end text (+ i (if triple? 3 1)) c triple?)\n                    [stack block?] (code stack)]\n                (case end\n                  :open (assoc state :string :open :string-at i)\n                  :broken (assoc state :error :string)\n                  (recur end stack line-begin false block?)))\n\n              (= \\\\ c)\n              (cond\n                (= (inc i) n) (assoc state :continued true)\n                (= \\newline (.charAt text (inc i)))\n                (recur (+ i 2) stack line-begin line? block?)\n                :else (let [[stack block?] (code stack)]\n                        (recur (inc i) stack line-begin false block?)))\n\n              (contains? #{\\( \\[ \\{} c)\n              (let [[stack block?] (code stack)]\n                (recur (inc i) (conj stack c) line-begin false block?))\n\n              (contains? opener-of c)\n              (if (= (peek stack) (opener-of c))\n                (let [[stack block?] (code stack)]\n                  (recur (inc i) (pop stack) line-begin false block?))\n                (assoc state :error :bracket))\n\n              (contains? #{\\space \\tab \\formfeed \\return} c)\n              (recur (inc i) stack line-begin line? block?)\n\n              :else\n              (let [[stack block?] (code stack)]\n                (recur (inc i) stack line-begin false block?)))))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Parse: where the first error failed\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- first-failure\n  \"Parse `source` and answer nil when it parses, `:end` when its first\n   error failed on an end-of-input token, else `:early`.\n\n   The failing token is the offending token of a mismatch; a\n   no-viable-alternative error names the token its prediction began at,\n   so for it the failing token is the furthest one the parser had read\n   when the error was reported. End-of-input tokens are EOF and the\n   zero-width ones after the last token with source text (the lexer's\n   EOF can carry a span, so it is excluded by type). A lexer error has no\n   token and is `:early`.\"\n  [^String source]\n  (let [{:keys [profile lexer-atn lexer-dfa lexer-cache parser-atn\n                parser-dfa parser-cache]}\n        (parser/make-worker)\n        far (volatile! -1)\n        failure (volatile! nil)\n        lexer ^Lexer ((:make-lexer profile) (CharStreams/fromString source))\n        tokens (proxy [CommonTokenStream] [lexer]\n                 (LT\n                   [k]\n                   (let [^Token t (proxy-super LT k)]\n                     (when t (vswap! far max (.getTokenIndex t)))\n                     t)))\n        listener (proxy [BaseErrorListener] []\n                   (syntaxError\n                     [_recognizer offending _line _column _msg e]\n                     (when-not @failure\n                       (vreset! failure\n                                (cond\n                                  (not (instance? Token offending)) :lexical\n                                  (instance? NoViableAltException e) @far\n                                  :else (.getTokenIndex ^Token offending))))))\n        _ (.setInterpreter\n            lexer\n            (LexerATNSimulator.\n              lexer\n              ^ATN lexer-atn\n              ^\"[Lorg.antlr.v4.runtime.dfa.DFA;\" lexer-dfa\n              ^PredictionContextCache lexer-cache))\n        _ (.removeErrorListeners lexer)\n        _ (.addErrorListener lexer ^ANTLRErrorListener listener)\n        parser ^Parser ((:make-parser profile) tokens)\n        _ (.setInterpreter\n            parser\n            (ParserATNSimulator.\n              parser\n              ^ATN parser-atn\n              ^\"[Lorg.antlr.v4.runtime.dfa.DFA;\" parser-dfa\n              ^PredictionContextCache parser-cache))\n        _ (.removeErrorListeners parser)\n        _ (.addErrorListener parser ^ANTLRErrorListener listener)]\n    ((:start profile) parser)\n    (let [index @failure\n          last-real (->> (.getTokens ^CommonTokenStream tokens)\n                         (keep (fn [^Token t]\n                                 (when (and (not= Token/EOF (.getType t))\n                                            (<= (.getStartIndex t)\n                                                (.getStopIndex t)))\n                                   (.getTokenIndex t))))\n                         (reduce max -1))]\n      (cond\n        (nil? index) nil\n        (and (int? index) (> index last-real)) :end\n        :else :early))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Probe\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- last-line-blank?\n  [text]\n  (str/blank? (peek (str/split text #\"\\n\" -1))))\n\n\n(defn probe\n  [text]\n  (let [{:keys [error string string-at continued brackets block?]} (scan text)\n        open? (boolean (or continued (seq brackets)))]\n    (cond\n      error :complete\n      ;; the open string stands as an empty one: an error on it or before\n      ;; it is not mended by more lines\n      string (if (= :early (first-failure (str (subs text 0 string-at)\n                                               \"''\\n\")))\n               :complete\n               :incomplete)\n      (and (not open?) (last-line-blank? text)) :complete\n      :else (case (first-failure (str text \"\\n\"))\n              nil (if (or open? block?) :incomplete :complete)\n              :end :incomplete\n              :early :complete))))\ncat: test/yang/antlr/cst_export_test.clj: No such file or directory\n{:tasks\n {gen:python-antlr\n  {:doc \"Generate and compile the pinned grammars-v4 Python3 parser into build/antlr/python3/classes (antlr/python3/manifest.edn)\"\n   :task (shell \"clojure -M:antlr-gen\")}\n\n  build:yin-repl-node\n  {:doc \"Compile the Node yin.repl (shadow-cljs build :yin-repl) to target/yin-repl.js\"\n   ;; yin.repl.dht-process-test spawns it as the JVM-to-Node reader of\n   ;; DHT epic S5, a required leg: a missing build fails that test.\n   :task (shell \"clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile yin-repl\")}\n\n  build:yin-repl-peer\n  {:doc \"Compile the yin.repl R5 cross-host peer (client role and --serve server role) as a Dart exe\"\n   ;; Process B of the R5 slice is a whole program rather than a test\n   ;; namespace, and this project's cljd.core carries the Flutter extensions,\n   ;; so only an ahead-of-time build is a peer the plain Dart VM can start.\n   ;; It is compiled alone and first: the test build rewrites the same\n   ;; cljd-out tree, so a peer built after it would import a tree it does\n   ;; not match.  The R5 cross-host tests spawn this one exe from the JVM\n   ;; and Node lanes, so `bb test` builds it first; a bare `bb test:clj` or\n   ;; `bb test:cljs` without it skips those tests with a printed notice\n   ;; rather than failing.\n   :task (do (shell \"mkdir -p build\")\n             (shell \"clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile yin.repl.slice-peer\")\n             (shell \"dart compile exe lib/cljd-out/yin/repl/slice-peer.dart -o build/yin-repl-peer\"))}\n\n  ;; Fast default lanes: no ^:slow test, no dao.test-slow/guard body.\n  test:clj {:doc \"JVM tests, fast: skips ^:slow ones; the DHT process test needs the Node REPL build\"\n            :depends [build:yin-repl-node gen:python-antlr]\n            :task (shell \"clojure -M:test -e :slow\")}\n\n  test:cljs {:doc \"Node tests, fast: dao.test-slow guarded bodies print SKIP; needs the Node REPL build\"\n             :depends [build:yin-repl-node]\n             :task (shell \"clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test\")}\n\n  test:cljd {:doc \"Dart tests, fast: aggregated shards via src/dev/cljd_agg.clj; guarded bodies print SKIP\"\n             :depends [build:yin-repl-peer]\n             :task (shell \"bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\")}\n\n  ;; Every test, slow ones included (about 20+ minutes on the JVM).\n  test:all:clj {:doc \"Every JVM test, ^:slow included (20+ minutes); for unattended runs\"\n                :depends [build:yin-repl-node gen:python-antlr]\n                :task (shell \"clojure -M:test\")}\n\n  test:all:cljs {:doc \"Every Node test, guarded slow bodies included (sets DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1)\"\n                 :depends [build:yin-repl-node]\n                 :task (shell {:extra-env {\"DATOM_SLOW_TESTS\" \"1\"}}\n                              \"clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test\")}\n\n  test:all:cljd {:doc \"Every Dart test, guarded slow bodies included (sets DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1)\"\n                 :depends [build:yin-repl-peer]\n                 :task (shell {:extra-env {\"DATOM_SLOW_TESTS\" \"1\"}}\n                              \"bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\")}\n\n  ;; Only the slow tests. Node and Dart select by namespace, so the other\n  ;; tests inside the nine namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard also run.\n  test:slow:clj {:doc \"Only the ^:slow JVM tests (about 14 minutes for long-loops-test); for unattended runs\"\n                 :depends [build:yin-repl-node gen:python-antlr]\n                 :task (shell \"clojure -M:test -i :slow\")}\n\n  test:slow:cljs {:doc \"Node: only namespaces with dao.test-slow/guard, bodies enabled (DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1)\"\n                  :depends [build:yin-repl-node]\n                  :task (let [re (-> (shell {:out :string}\n                                            \"bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --slow-regex\")\n                                     :out\n                                     clojure.string/trim)]\n                          (shell {:extra-env {\"DATOM_SLOW_TESTS\" \"1\"}}\n                                 \"clj\" \"-M:cljs\" \"-m\" \"shadow.cljs.devtools.cli\"\n                                 \"compile\" \"test\" \"--config-merge\"\n                                 (str \"{:ns-regexp \\\"\" re \"\\\"}\")))}\n\n  test:slow:cljd {:doc \"Dart: only generated tests of namespaces with dao.test-slow/guard, bodies enabled (DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1)\"\n                  :depends [build:yin-repl-peer]\n                  :task (shell {:extra-env {\"DATOM_SLOW_TESTS\" \"1\"}}\n                               \"bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --slow-only\")}\n\n  test:slow {:doc \"Only the slow tests on every lane: JVM by tag, Node and Dart by namespace\"\n             :depends [test:slow:clj test:slow:cljs test:slow:cljd]}\n\n  test:all {:doc \"Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM, Node, Dart)\"\n            :depends [test:all:clj test:all:cljs test:all:cljd]}\n\n  test {:doc \"All three lanes in their fast default form (JVM, Node, Dart)\"\n        :depends [build:yin-repl-peer build:yin-repl-node test:clj test:cljs test:cljd]}\n\n  ;; Only the tests a change can affect (src/dev/affected.clj): the\n  ;; reverse require closure of the changed files since the merge-base\n  ;; with master. Extra args pass through (--base REF, --changed FILE...).\n  ;; Exit 2 on a wide change (deps.edn, bb.edn, src/dev/, ...): run bb test.\n  test:changed {:doc \"Fast tests a change can affect, three lanes in parallel\"\n                :task (System/exit\n                        (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                      \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                      *command-line-args*)))}\n\n  test:changed:clj {:doc \"JVM lane of test:changed\"\n                    :task (System/exit\n                            (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                          \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                          \"--lane\" \"clj\" *command-line-args*)))}\n\n  test:changed:cljs {:doc \"Node lane of test:changed\"\n                     :task (System/exit\n                             (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                           \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                           \"--lane\" \"cljs\"\n                                           *command-line-args*)))}\n\n  test:changed:cljd {:doc \"Dart lane of test:changed\"\n                     :task (System/exit\n                             (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                           \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                           \"--lane\" \"cljd\"\n                                           *command-line-args*)))}\n\n  test:changed:list {:doc \"What test:changed would run and why; runs nothing\"\n                     :task (System/exit\n                             (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                           \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                           \"--list\" *command-line-args*)))}\n\n  ;; Subsystem tests (src/dev/affected.clj): canonical subsystems defined\n  ;; in src/dev/subsystems.edn. Positional arguments name subsystems; flags\n  ;; pass through (--lane L, --list, --slow).\n  test:sub {:doc \"Subsystem tests, three lanes in parallel: bb test:sub <subsystem>... [--slow]\"\n            :task (let [[subs flags] (split-with (fn [arg] (not (clojure.string/starts-with? arg \"--\")))\n                                                 *command-line-args*)\n                        sub-args (mapcat (fn [s] [\"--subsystem\" s]) subs)]\n                    (System/exit\n                      (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                    \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                    (concat sub-args flags)))))}\n\n  test:sub:clj {:doc \"JVM lane of test:sub: bb test:sub:clj <subsystem>... [--slow]\"\n                :task (let [[subs flags] (split-with (fn [arg] (not (clojure.string/starts-with? arg \"--\")))\n                                                     *command-line-args*)\n                            sub-args (mapcat (fn [s] [\"--subsystem\" s]) subs)]\n                        (System/exit\n                          (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                        \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                        \"--lane\" \"clj\"\n                                        (concat sub-args flags)))))}\n\n  test:sub:cljs {:doc \"Node lane of test:sub: bb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>... [--slow]\"\n                 :task (let [[subs flags] (split-with (fn [arg] (not (clojure.string/starts-with? arg \"--\")))\n                                                      *command-line-args*)\n                             sub-args (mapcat (fn [s] [\"--subsystem\" s]) subs)]\n                         (System/exit\n                           (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                         \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                         \"--lane\" \"cljs\"\n                                         (concat sub-args flags)))))}\n\n  test:sub:cljd {:doc \"Dart lane of test:sub: bb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>... [--slow]\"\n                 :task (let [[subs flags] (split-with (fn [arg] (not (clojure.string/starts-with? arg \"--\")))\n                                                      *command-line-args*)\n                             sub-args (mapcat (fn [s] [\"--subsystem\" s]) subs)]\n                         (System/exit\n                           (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                         \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                         \"--lane\" \"cljd\"\n                                         (concat sub-args flags)))))}\n\n  test:sub:list {:doc \"What test:sub would run, prerequisites and seams: bb test:sub:list <subsystem>...\"\n                 :task (let [[subs flags] (split-with (fn [arg] (not (clojure.string/starts-with? arg \"--\")))\n                                                      *command-line-args*)\n                             sub-args (mapcat (fn [s] [\"--subsystem\" s]) subs)]\n                         (System/exit\n                           (:exit (apply shell {:continue true}\n                                         \"bb\" \"src/dev/affected.clj\"\n                                         \"--list\"\n                                         (concat sub-args flags)))))}\n  }}\n{:deps {org.clojure/core.async {:mvn/version \"1.8.741\"}\n        org.clojure/tools.reader {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}\n        ring/ring {:mvn/version \"1.14.1\"}\n        com.cognitect/transit-clj {:mvn/version \"1.0.333\"}\n        org.replikativ/boring {:mvn/version \"0.1.30\"}\n        com.datomic/peer {:mvn/version \"1.0.7364\"}\n        org.postgresql/postgresql {:mvn/version \"42.7.3\"}\n        org.clojure/data.json {:mvn/version \"2.5.0\"}\n        http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \"2.8.0\"}\n        org.jsoup/jsoup {:mvn/version \"1.18.3\"}\n        instaparse/instaparse {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}\n        ;; yang.python.antlr: the runtime the generated Python3 parser runs\n        ;; on; pinned to the tool version in :antlr-gen (they are one release)\n        org.antlr/antlr4-runtime {:mvn/version \"4.13.2\"}\n        criterium/criterium {:mvn/version \"0.4.6\"}\n        io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3 {:mvn/version \"1.3\"}\n        stigmergy/chp {:mvn/version \"0.1.0-SNAPSHOT\"\n                       :exclusions [refactor-nrepl/refactor-nrepl]}}\n :cljd/opts {}\n ;; build/antlr/python3/classes: the generated Python3 parser, produced by\n ;; `clj -M:antlr-gen` (antlr/python3/manifest.edn); absent until generated\n :paths [\"src/clj\" \"src/cljc\" \"src/cljs\" \"src/cljd\"\n         \"build/antlr/python3/classes\"]\n :aliases {:clean {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                   :exec-fn clean/clean}\n           :profile-btree {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                           :extra-deps {persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\n                                        {:mvn/version \"0.3.0\"}}\n                           :main-opts [\"-m\" \"btree-bench\"]}\n           :test {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                  :extra-deps {io.github.cognitect-labs/test-runner\n                               {:git/tag \"v0.5.1\" :git/sha \"dfb30dd\"}}\n                  :main-opts [\"-m\" \"cognitect.test-runner\"]\n                  :exec-fn cognitect.test-runner.api/test}\n           :nREPL {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                   :extra-deps {nrepl/nrepl {:mvn/version \"1.0.0\"}\n                                cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \"0.58.0\"}}\n                   :main-opts [\"-m\" \"nrepl.cmdline\" \"--middleware\" \"[cider.nrepl/cider-middleware]\"]}\n           :cljs {:extra-paths [\"test\" \"src/dev\"]\n                  :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                               reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                               com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}}}\n           :dev {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                 :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                              cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \"0.58.0\"}\n                              reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                              com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}\n                              ;; benchmark baseline + psset-fixtures\n                              ;; generator only; production uses\n                              ;; dao.data.btree\n                              persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\n                              {:mvn/version \"0.3.0\"}}\n                 :jvm-opts [\"-Dconfig=config.clj\"]}\n           ;; The one ClojureDart pin. Compose it with the build aliases:\n           ;; clj -M:clojuredart:cljd ..., clj -M:clojuredart:cljd-yin-repl-build ...\n           :clojuredart {:extra-deps {sonwh98/clojuredart\n                                      {:git/url \"https://github.com/sonwh98/ClojureDart.git\"\n                                       :sha \"7d00ee2aa7fa9d990581e78ebc4a66b93cb70a31\"}\n                                      }}\n           :cljd {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                  :main-opts [\"-m\" \"cljd.build\"]\n                  :cljd/opts {:kind :flutter :main datomworld.demo.main}}\n           :server {:extra-deps {http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \"2.8.0\"}}\n                    :jvm-opts [\"-Dconfig=config.clj\"]\n                    :main-opts [\"-m\" \"world.server\"]}\n           :clj-yin-repl {:main-opts [\"-m\" \"yin.repl.main\"]}\n           :cljs-yin-repl\n           {:extra-paths [\"test\" \"src/dev\"]\n            :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                         reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                         com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}}\n            :main-opts [\"-m\" \"shadow.cljs.devtools.cli\" \"run\" \"yin.repl.main/-main\"]}\n           :fmt {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                 :exec-fn tasks/fix-all}\n           ;; Build-time generation of the grammars-v4 Python3 parser.\n           ;; The tool version must equal the antlr4-runtime above.\n           :antlr-gen {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                       :extra-deps {org.antlr/antlr4 {:mvn/version \"4.13.2\"}}\n                       :main-opts [\"-m\" \"yang-antlr-gen\"]}\n           :kondo {:extra-deps {clj-kondo/clj-kondo {:mvn/version \"2026.01.19\"}}\n                   :main-opts [\"-m\" \"clj-kondo.main\"]}\n           :dbg {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                 :main-opts [\"-m\" \"debug-cljd\"]\n                 :cljd/opts {:kind :flutter :main datomworld.demo.main}}\n           :cljd-yin-repl {:main-opts [\"-m\" \"yin.repl.runner\"]}\n           :cljd-yin-repl-build\n           {:main-opts  [\"-m\" \"cljd.build\"]\n            :cljd/opts  {:kind       :dart\n                         :main       yin.repl.main\n                         :output-dir \"build/yin-repl\"\n                         :output-to  \"build/yin-repl/yin_repl.dart\"}}\n           :dep-graph {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                       :extra-deps {org.clojure/tools.namespace {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}}\n                       :main-opts [\"-m\" \"dep-graph\"]}}}\n\n\n;; clj -X:fmt\n\n;; clj -M:nREPL\n\n;; clj -M:nREPL:server\n;; clj -M:test\n;; clj -M:test -n yin.vm-literal-test\n;; clj -M:test -n yin.vm-continuation-test\n;; clj -M:test -n yang.python-test\n;; clj -X:test :nses '[yin.vm-literal-test yin.vm-test]'\n;; clj -M:test -v yin.vm-addition-test/test-lambda-with-multiple-operations\n\n;; clj -X:clean\n\n;; npx shadow-cljs compile test && node target/node-tests.js\n;; npx shadow-cljs release demo\n;; clj -M:dev -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch demo\n;; clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch demo\n;; clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile demo\n;; clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release demo\n\n\n;; mise exec -- clj -M:clojuredart:cljd watch \n;; mise exec -- flutter run \n\n;; To start the emulator:                                                                                              ;; emulator -avd Pixel_3_API_34\n;; flutter emulators --launch Pixel_3_API_34\n;; emulator -avd Pixel_3_API_34 -no-snapshot-load  \n;; emulator -list-avds\n\n;;  clj -M:dep-graph                    # full report + dep-graph.dot + dep-graph.svg\n;;  clj -M:dep-graph --filter \"yin.*\"   # only yin namespaces\n;;  clj -M:dep-graph --layout circo     # choose graphviz layout engine\n(ns yang.frontend\n  \"The language frontend catalog (docs/design/yang.antlr.md section 3,\n   section 8.5.6 slice F1): manifest validation, `install`, and `select`.\n\n   A catalog is a plain immutable value. There is no global registry and\n   no namespace-load registration: `install` returns a new catalog and\n   never changes its argument, so an in-flight request that pinned a\n   catalog snapshot keeps its behavior (section 3.3).\n\n   A manifest is portable data only: no functions, host handles, records,\n   or other host objects anywhere in it (section 3.1). The installed\n   binding is the host's own value, kept beside the manifest and never\n   inspected here; its shape is the composition's (slice F2).\n\n   Revisions of one frontend id coexist (section 3.7): an entry is keyed by\n   `[id revision]`, and a revision is immutable once installed.\n\n   Every refusal is a returned data outcome,\n   `{:status :refused, :reason :yang.frontend/<reason>}` plus its evidence;\n   a selection is `{:status :ok, :manifest m, :binding b}`.\")\n\n\n(def supported-spi\n  \"The SPI revisions (`:yang.frontend/spi`) this catalog admits.\"\n  #{1})\n\n\n(def refusal-reasons\n  \"Every reason a catalog refusal may carry.\"\n  #{:yang.frontend/malformed :yang.frontend/non-portable\n    :yang.frontend/incompatible-spi :yang.frontend/revision-conflict\n    :yang.frontend/unknown :yang.frontend/ambiguous\n    :yang.frontend/incompatible})\n\n\n(defn refused\n  \"A qualified refusal: `{:status :refused, :reason reason}` merged with\n   the evidence `data`.\"\n  ([reason] (refused reason {}))\n  ([reason data]\n   (merge data {:status :refused, :reason reason})))\n\n\n(defn refused?\n  \"True when `res` is a catalog refusal.\"\n  [res]\n  (and (map? res) (= :refused (:status res))))\n\n\n(defn ok?\n  \"True when `res` is a successful selection.\"\n  [res]\n  (and (map? res) (= :ok (:status res))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Manifest validation (sections 3.1, 3.7)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- non-portable-path\n  \"The path to the first value in `x` that is not portable data, or nil.\n   A set member has no stable position, so its path ends at the set, and a\n   map key's path ends at its map. With several defects the first reported\n   follows host iteration order.\"\n  [x path]\n  (cond (or (nil? x) (boolean? x) (string? x) (keyword? x) (symbol? x)\n            (number? x) (char? x))\n        nil\n        (record? x) path\n        (map? x) (some (fn [[k v]]\n                         (or (non-portable-path k path)\n                             (non-portable-path v (conj path k))))\n                       x)\n        (or (vector? x) (seq? x))\n        (some (fn [[i v]] (non-portable-path v (conj path i)))\n              (map-indexed vector x))\n        (set? x) (when (some #(non-portable-path % path) x) path)\n        :else path))\n\n\n(defn- address?\n  \"A content address: a `:segment/...` key or a non-empty string.\"\n  [x]\n  (or (keyword? x) (and (string? x) (seq x))))\n\n\n(def ^:private manifest-checks\n  \"`[key valid?]` for every key section 3.7 pins per request. The spi is\n   `int?`, which on JavaScript also admits an integral float such as 1.0.\"\n  [[:yang.frontend/id qualified-keyword?]\n   [:yang.frontend/spi int?]\n   [:yang.frontend/revision #(and (string? %) (seq %))]\n   [:yang.frontend/language keyword?]\n   [:yang.frontend/grammar map?]\n   [:yang.frontend/options-schema address?]\n   [:yang.frontend/lowering-profile address?]\n   [:yang.frontend/runtime-profile address?]\n   [:yang.frontend/support-profile address?]])\n\n\n(def ^:private grammar-checks\n  \"`[key valid?]` for the grammar package. The lexer and parser names are\n   ANTLR's and optional, but a non-empty string when present: the SPI does\n   not require ANTLR (section 3.6).\"\n  [[:yang.grammar/package address?]\n   [:yang.grammar/entries #(and (map? %) (seq %))]\n   [:yang.grammar/export-profile keyword?]])\n\n\n(defn- optional-name?\n  [m k]\n  (or (not (contains? m k))\n      (let [v (get m k)] (and (string? v) (seq v)))))\n\n\n(defn- first-defect\n  [m checks path]\n  (some (fn [[k valid?]]\n          (let [v (get m k)]\n            (when-not (valid? v)\n              (refused :yang.frontend/malformed\n                       {:path (conj path k),\n                        :defect (if (contains? m k) :invalid :missing)}))))\n        checks))\n\n\n(defn validate\n  \"`{:status :ok, :manifest m}` when `m` is a portable, versioned manifest\n   this catalog admits, else the refusal naming the first defect.\"\n  [m]\n  (if-not (map? m)\n    (refused :yang.frontend/malformed {:path [], :defect :invalid})\n    (if-let [path (non-portable-path m [])]\n      (refused :yang.frontend/non-portable {:path path})\n      (or (first-defect m manifest-checks [])\n          (first-defect (:yang.frontend/grammar m)\n                        grammar-checks\n                        [:yang.frontend/grammar])\n          (some (fn [k]\n                  (when-not (optional-name? (:yang.frontend/grammar m) k)\n                    (refused :yang.frontend/malformed\n                             {:path [:yang.frontend/grammar k],\n                              :defect :invalid})))\n                [:yang.grammar/lexer :yang.grammar/parser])\n          (when-not (contains? supported-spi (:yang.frontend/spi m))\n            (refused :yang.frontend/incompatible-spi\n                     {:spi (:yang.frontend/spi m),\n                      :supported supported-spi}))\n          {:status :ok, :manifest m}))))\n\n(defn- select-frontend\n  \"Select the frontend `lang` names in `catalog`: a qualified id selects\n   id (ambiguous when several revisions are installed), `[id revision]`\n   pins one revision, any other keyword selects the one id installed for\n   that language.\"\n  [catalog lang]\n  (cond\n    (vector? lang) (frontend/select catalog\n                                    {:yang.frontend/id (first lang),\n                                     :yang.frontend/revision (second lang)})\n    (qualified-keyword? lang) (frontend/select catalog\n                                               {:yang.frontend/id lang})\n    :else (let [ids (language-ids catalog lang)]\n            (case (count ids)\n              0 (frontend/refused :yang.frontend/unknown {:language lang})\n              1 (frontend/select catalog {:yang.frontend/id (first ids)})\n              (frontend/refused :yang.frontend/ambiguous {:ids ids})))))\n\n\n(defn- usable-binding\n  \"The binding of a successful `selected`, when it has a parse and a lower\n   stage, else nil.\"\n  [selected]\n  (let [binding (when (frontend/ok? selected) (:binding selected))]\n    (when (and (map? binding) (fn? (:parse binding)) (fn? (:lower binding)))\n      binding)))\n\n\n(defn- frontend-binding\n  \"The installed binding for `lang` in the session's pinned catalog.  With\n   no parse and lower stage installed it throws an `unavailable-parser`\n   diagnostic: there is no fallback to another frontend.\"\n  [state lang]\n  (let [selected (select-frontend (:frontends state) lang)]\n    (or (usable-binding selected)\n        (throw (ex-info (str \"unavailable-parser: no parser for \" (pr-str lang))\n                        {:diagnostic :unavailable-parser\n                         :lang lang\n                         :selection selected})))))\n\n\n(defn- lower-parsed\n  \"Lower `parsed` through `binding`.  A binding with a `:lower-session`\n   stage lowers against the frontend session the shell keeps\n   (`:frontend-session`, cleared with the VM) and leaves the session it\n   answers, with the binding's `:display` policy, in the program's\n{:grammar {:repo \"antlr/grammars-v4\",\n           :path \"python/python3\",\n           :revision \"8af0d4c26c796ea27c15c3d85418f2d0f77c3adb\",\n           ;; The two MIT grammars are vendored verbatim beside this manifest\n           ;; (owner decision \"Vendor .g4 only\"); the two Java helpers, whose\n           ;; licence is unresolved, are still fetched at the pinned revision.\n           ;; Every file is digest-checked either way.\n           :files {\"Python3Lexer.g4\"\n                   {:blake3 \"407a5312b637ebe386eabaceca758a5714f1d3e26b1f058b96e43dca3ec02662\",\n                    :vendored \"antlr/python3/Python3Lexer.g4\"},\n                   \"Python3Parser.g4\"\n                   {:blake3 \"e82099eb8fda51ea8b49c266cba8eb2af47eb400fe657d8d8867b1eaf62fe00d\",\n                    :vendored \"antlr/python3/Python3Parser.g4\"},\n                   \"Java/Python3LexerBase.java\"\n                   {:blake3 \"5212b67286df5ec7f8818876cbb2bb178e80fcdc5b3daae69e225edd5f68d385\"},\n                   \"Java/Python3ParserBase.java\"\n                   {:blake3 \"f29fa8fc257027885afdd9c778add55a1128c83b0f888035904d952be0e5222d\"}},\n           :generate [\"Python3Lexer.g4\" \"Python3Parser.g4\"]},\n :tool {:antlr \"4.13.2\", :runtime \"org.antlr/antlr4-runtime 4.13.2\"},\n :target :java,\n :package \"yang.python.antlr.gen\",\n :flags [\"-Dlanguage=Java\" \"-package\" \"yang.python.antlr.gen\" \"-no-listener\"\n         \"-no-visitor\"],\n ;; Every Java helper gets `package yang.python.antlr.gen;` prepended.\n ;; Exact-text replacements, applied in order after that. Upstream builds\n ;; NEWLINE and DEDENT with CommonToken(start, stop) at the end of the\n ;; newline-plus-indentation lexeme and never sets their text, so ANTLR reads\n ;; their text back from whatever source sits there: a NEWLINE before an\n ;; INDENT reads a space, and a DEDENT reads the newline it follows. The patch\n ;; puts NEWLINE on its newline characters and makes DEDENT zero-width\n ;; (stop = start - 1), so every token's span is the source it came from.\n :patches\n {\"Java/Python3LexerBase.java\"\n  [[\"            emit(commonToken(Python3Lexer.NEWLINE, newLine));\"\n    \"            emit(positionedToken(Python3Lexer.NEWLINE, this._tokenStartCharIndex, this._tokenStartCharIndex + newLine.length() - 1));\"]\n   [\"        CommonToken dedent = commonToken(Python3Lexer.DEDENT, \\\"\\\");\"\n    \"        CommonToken dedent = positionedToken(Python3Lexer.DEDENT, this.getCharIndex(), this.getCharIndex() - 1);\"]\n   [\"            this.emit(commonToken(Python3Lexer.NEWLINE, \\\"\\\\n\\\"));\"\n    \"            this.emit(positionedToken(Python3Lexer.NEWLINE, this.getCharIndex(), this.getCharIndex() - 1));\"]\n   [\"    private CommonToken commonToken(int type, String text) {\"\n    \"    private CommonToken positionedToken(int type, int start, int stop) {\\n        return new CommonToken(this._tokenFactorySourcePair, type, DEFAULT_TOKEN_CHANNEL, start, stop);\\n    }\\n\\n    private CommonToken commonToken(int type, String text) {\"]]},\n ;; BLAKE3 of every generated/patched .java file; generation fails on drift\n :generated {\"Python3Lexer.java\"\n             \"bb61a463af3d2c5d3f58778c0b10e082f1b3c88fd793102646e632087a540848\",\n             \"Python3LexerBase.java\"\n             \"1c8ac0a2bd1cc1499b842e251eea74a13cf7b6e984ece6cb5b55ff69403b354e\",\n             \"Python3Parser.java\"\n             \"8ba1f2398e52994e8ead1d68768c723bed6dbd4b5eccd48d57d585d1684f13d7\",\n             \"Python3ParserBase.java\"\n             \"332745797ab6423d234c796e80103823d718bc9da0a73643682a43d0c42c45be\"},\n ;; §4.5 license review\n :license {\"Python3Lexer.g4\" \"MIT (header: Copyright (c) 2014 Bart Kiers); vendored with its notice verbatim\",\n           \"Python3Parser.g4\" \"MIT (header: Copyright (c) 2014 Bart Kiers); vendored with its notice verbatim\",\n           \"Java/Python3LexerBase.java\" \"no notice in file; UNRESOLVED: not vendored, fetched by pinned digest\",\n           \"Java/Python3ParserBase.java\" \"no notice in file; UNRESOLVED: not vendored, fetched by pinned digest\",\n           \"antlr4-runtime 4.13.2\" \"BSD-3-Clause\"},\n ;; Generated artifacts include code derived from the two helpers, so their\n ;; provenance must be resolved before any generated artifact is distributed.\n :redistribution {:vendored-sources [\"antlr/python3/Python3Lexer.g4\"\n                                     \"antlr/python3/Python3Parser.g4\"],\n                  :generated-artifacts :none,\n                  :blocked-until \"Java helper provenance and licence resolved\"}}\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
